equation below gives the area of the shaded region. From *left to right*, what numbers are missing?\n\n$0$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073550 1072 #uuid "89b8eab6-98e4-5197-9c04-e4b4e7dde043" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073550 1074 "First, notice the unit square is divided into $3$ equal slices horizontally and into $4$ slices vertically. So, each of the small tiles is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nunits long and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nunits high.\n\nThe shaded region is $2$ tiles long and $3$ tiles high, which means that it has dimensions\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nunits by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nunits.\n\n![](images/de/defd66d84d10e351b131828d86d26c04bdf2792aa003355e5cf6910e65d1cff7.png)\n\nWe find the area of the shaded rectangle by multiplying the lengths of the sides:\n$2$\n\nSo, from left to right, the missing numbers are ${\\color{blue}2},{\\color{red}4}$ and $\\color{magenta}6$." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073551 1069 "e-2039" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073551 1071 "What is $7.2$ divided by $0.6$?" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073551 1072 #uuid "3c5626bf-2c51-5154-a1e9-63e7325a9f8f" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073551 1074 "First, we write the division problem as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7.2}{0.6}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $10$ so that both become whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7.2\\times 10}{0.6\\times 10} = \\dfrac{72}{6}\n$$\n\nSo the problem is equivalent to $72\\div 6$. We will use long division:\n\n| $6$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the tens:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$6$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}7} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$1$$\\color{lightgray}2$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}7} \\div 6 = {\\color{blue}1}\\,\\text{R}\\,1$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $6 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 6$Subtract: ${\\color{red}7} - 6 = 1$Bring down: $2$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the ones:\n\n| $1$$\\color{blue}2$$6$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}2$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$\\fbox{0}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}12} \\div 6 = {\\color{blue}2}$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}2$Multiply: $6 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 12$Subtract: ${\\color{red}12} - 12 = \\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits, so the division is done.\n\nWe find that\n\n$$\n72 \\div 6 = 12\n$$\n\nso we conclude that\n\n$$\n7.2 \\div 0.6 = 12 \\,\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073552 1069 "e-2040" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073552 1071 "What is $86.5 \\div 0.5$?" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073552 1072 #uuid "15aeeda1-5a03-5476-84d5-88c2971ad8f5" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073552 1074 "First, we write the division problem as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{86.5}{0.5}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $10$ so that both become whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{86.5\\times 10}{0.5\\times 10} = \\dfrac{865}{5}\n$$\n\nSo the problem is equivalent to $865\\div 5$. We will use long division:\n\n| $5$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$ |\n| --- |\n\nCarrying out the long division process as usual, we get:\n\n| $1$$7$$3$$5$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$3$$6$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$5$$1$$5$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$5$${0}$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits, so the division is done.\n\nWe find that\n\n$$\n865 \\div 5 = 173\n$$\n\nso we conclude that\n\n$$\n86.5 \\div 0.5 = 173 \\,\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073553 1069 "e-2042" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073553 1071 "What is $9.45$ divided by $0.09$?" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073553 1072 #uuid "5145d781-15c7-5bc9-8389-19334f821d94" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073553 1074 "First, we write the division problem as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9.45}{0.09}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply the numerator and denominator by a power of $10$ so that both become whole numbers. Here, there are $2$ decimal places, so we need to multiply by $100$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9.45\\times 100}{0.09\\times 100} = \\dfrac{945}{9}\n$$\n\nSo the problem is equivalent to $945\\div 9$. We will use long division:\n\n| $9$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$ |\n| --- |\n\nApplying the long division process as usual, we get:\n\n| $1$$0$$5$$9$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$0$$4$$5$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$5$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits, so the division is done.\n\nWe found that\n\n$$\n945 \\div 9 = 105\n$$\n\nso we conclude that\n\n$$\n9.45 \\div 0.09 = 105 \\,\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073554 1069 "e-2047" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073554 1071 "Abby wants to cut a $63$ inch piece of wire into four smaller pieces of equal size. How long should each piece be? Round your answer to $1$ decimal place." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073554 1072 #uuid "8a6dbba4-96c3-5f39-b0d3-abc9cfeab392" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073554 1074 "To determine the length of each piece, we need to divide $63$ by $4$.\n\nWe start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\, 1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5 {\\,. \\,} 7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{blue}5} \\:$$4$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\,. \\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!$$4$$2$$3$$-\\!\\!\\!$$2$$0$$3$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$2$$8$$2$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$2$$0$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe went through all the digits and reached a remainder of $0$, so the division is done.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n63 \\div 4= 15.75\n$$\n\nFinally, we round the quotient to the nearest tenth ($1$ decimal place). The hundredths place is $5$, which is greater than or equal to $5$, so we round up:\n\n$$\n15.75 \\rightarrow 15.8\n$$\n\nWe conclude that each piece would be about $15.8$ inches long." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073555 1069 "e-2048" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073555 1071 "Round the value of $137 \\div 8$ to $1$ decimal place." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073555 1072 #uuid "e6332afa-2566-5a36-89d1-ae127b6c662e" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073555 1074 "We start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\,\\, \\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\,. \\,} 1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{black}2} \\: \\ldots$$8$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\,. \\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$5$$7$$-\\!\\!\\!$$5$$6$$1$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$8$$2$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$1$$6$$4$$\\vdots$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe could divide further, but since we want to round to $1$ decimal place, we stop after finding the quotient to $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n137 \\div 8 = 17.12\\ldots\n$$\n\nFinally, we round the quotient to the nearest tenth ($1$ decimal place). The hundredths place is $2$, which is less than $5$, so we round down:\n$17.12\\ldots \\rightarrow 17.1\\,$." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073556 1069 "e-2049" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073556 1071 "Round the value of $66 \\div 9$ to the nearest hundredth." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073556 1072 #uuid "3a834cf6-d46e-551e-a1c3-7649e781d088" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073556 1074 "We start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\,\\, \\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\,. \\,} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{blue}3}\\: \\ldots$$9$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\,. \\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$3$$3$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$2$$7$$3$$0$$-$$2$$7$$3$$0$$-\\!\\!$$2$$7$$3$$\\vdots$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe could divide further, but since we want to round to $2$ decimal places (the nearest hundredth), we stop after finding the quotient to $3$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n66 \\div 9 = 7.333\\ldots\n$$\n\nFinally, we round the quotient to the nearest hundredth. The thousandths place is $3$, which is less than $5$, so we round down:\n$7.333\\ldots \\rightarrow 7.33\\,$." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073557 1069 "e-2051" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073557 1071 "What is $54.8 \\div 4$?" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073557 1072 #uuid "96972378-c7c1-55e3-b333-eeeabb10e5ad" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073557 1074 "We will use the standard algorithm.\n\nFirst, we write down the problem using long division notation, keeping the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $4$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 8}$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe also bring the decimal point up into the quotient, placing it directly above the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $\\,\\, \\,\\phantom{0} \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}$$4$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 8}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow, we carry out the long division process as usual:\n\n| $\\,\\, 1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\,\\,. \\,} 7$$4$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\,\\,. \\,} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$1$$4$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$2$$8$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$8$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n54.8 \\div 4 = 13.7 \\,\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073558 1069 "e-2052" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073558 1071 "To make $4$ servings of ice cream, Rita used $3.4$ ounces of cocoa powder. How much cocoa powder did she use for each serving?" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073558 1072 #uuid "a622f6ba-cf23-59ee-951b-1c21654983a7" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073558 1074 "To determine the amount of cocoa powder Rita used to make each serving, we need to divide $3.4$ by $4$. To do this, we can use the standard algorithm.\n\nFirst, we write down the problem using long division notation, keeping the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $4$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 4}{\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe also bring the decimal point up into the quotient, placing it directly above the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $\\:\\; \\phantom{0} \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}$$4$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 4}{\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow, we carry out the long division process as usual:\n\n| $\\, 0 {\\,\\,. \\,} 8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}5$$4$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{\\, 3 {\\,\\,. \\,} 4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}0}$$\\,-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$2$$2$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$0$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3.4 \\div 4 = 0.85 \\,\n$$\n\nWe conclude that Rita used $0.85$ ounces of cocoa powder to make each serving of ice cream." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073559 1069 "e-2054" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073559 1071 "$0.0694 \\div 0.2=$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073559 1072 #uuid "8060f93b-78f8-5d31-ad4d-6c44674961b1" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073559 1074 "We can write this division problem as a fraction, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.0694}{0.2}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the numerator and denominator by $10$, we get a whole number in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.0694 \\times 10}{0.2\\times 10} = \\dfrac{0.694}{2}\n$$\n\nNow we see that the division problem is equivalent to $0.694 \\div 2$. We can compute this using long division as usual:\n\n| $0 {\\,. \\,} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7$$2$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{0 {\\,. \\,} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!$$6$$0$$9$$-\\!\\!\\!$$8$$1$$4$$-\\!\\!\\!$$1$$4$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nFrom the long division, we find that\n\n$$\n0.694 \\div 2 = 0.347\n$$\n\nso we conclude that\n\n$$\n0.0694 \\div 0.2 = 0.347 \\,\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073560 1069 "e-2055" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073560 1071 "What is $0.471 \\div 0.02$?" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073560 1072 #uuid "36b548ff-acf9-52e4-a253-db809625bdae" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073560 1074 "We can write this division problem as a fraction, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.471}{0.02}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the numerator and denominator by $100$, we get a whole number in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.471 \\times 100}{0.02\\times 100} = \\dfrac{47.1}{2}\n$$\n\nNow we see that the division problem is equivalent to $47.1 \\div 2$. We can compute this using the standard algorithm:\n\n| $\\,2\\,\\,$$\\,3\\,$.$\\,5\\,\\,$$\\,5\\,\\,$$2$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}\\,} \\!\\!\\!.\\, \\!1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!$$4$$0$$7$$-\\!\\!\\!$$6$$1$$1$$-\\!\\!\\!$$1$$0$$1$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$1$$0$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nFrom the long division, we find that\n\n$$\n47.1 \\div 2 = 23.55\n$$\n\nso we conclude that\n\n$$\n0.471 \\div 0.02 = 23.55 \\,\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073561 1069 "e-21985" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073561 1071 "Find the area of the triangle.\n\n![](images/4b/4bfa4b996e3076298faeddd53b177df0304826a00d262cc4e0c37a0478b5c76e.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073561 1072 #uuid "65268d2f-3b10-50ea-91cb-c4407e6201c2" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073561 1074 "The area of a right triangle is half the area of the corresponding rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area of Triangle} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot \\text{(Area of Rectangle)}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the corresponding rectangle has dimensions $10\\:\\text{m}$ by $6\\:\\text{m}$.\n\n![](images/80/806dd76afcf6f5382ee4a1eb63adb9cbb0ef37c8e47d1eed61b31e774d7d08df.png)\n\nThe area of the rectangle is\n\n$$\n\\text{Area of Rectangle} = 10 \\cdot 6 = 60 \\: \\text{m}^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\nArea of Triangle = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 60 = 30m^{2}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073562 1069 "e-22001" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073562 1071 "A flowerbed in the shape of a right triangle has a base length of $10\\,\\textrm{ft}$ and a width of $7\\,\\textrm{ft}$. What is the area of the flowerbed?" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073562 1072 #uuid "29c773c6-f5e4-5c44-8623-43cb95022947" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073562 1074 "Let's sketch our flowerbed based on the description.\n\n![](images/d9/d9199454ba5ead36674d3bb3bc97ea972817f218d5cad8168390dd3acdf17f1f.png)\n\nThe area of a right triangle is half the area of the corresponding rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area of Triangle} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot \\text{(Area of Rectangle)}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the corresponding rectangle has dimensions $10\\:\\text{ft}$ by $7\\:\\text{ft}$.\nSo, the area of the rectangle is\n\n$$\n\\text{Area of Rectangle} = 10 \\cdot 7 = 70 \\: \\text{ft}^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\nArea of Triangle = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 70 = 35ft^{2}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073563 1069 "e-22002" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073563 1071 "At two music schools, School A has $8$ instructors and $24$ students, while School B has $18$ instructors and $27$ students. Fill in the missing details in the explanations below.\n\n$\\quad$ The number of instructors relative to students for School A is $\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid gray]{\\phantom{1/3}}$.\n\n$\\quad$ The number of instructors relative to students for School B is $\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid gray]{\\phantom{2/3}}$.\n\n$\\quad$ Therefore, School A has a $\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid gray]{\\phantom{\\textrm{smaller}}}$ fraction of instructors relative to students compared to School B." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073563 1072 #uuid "8eb7c887-91a2-5ecf-ba97-63747b1340c2" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073563 1074 "The number of instructors relative to students for School A is the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{24}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing both the numerator and the denominator by $8{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8 \\div 8}{24 \\div 8} = \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nThe number of instructors relative to students for School B is the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{18}{27}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing both the numerator and the denominator by $8{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{18 \\div 9}{27 \\div 9} = \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTo compare\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nwe look at the numerators since the denominators are the same.\n\nSince $1 < 2$, the fraction for School A is *smaller.*\n\nThe complete statements are as follows:\n\n$\\quad$ The number of instructors relative to students for School A is\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid gray]{\\dfrac{1}{3}}\n$$\n\n$\\quad$ The number of instructors relative to students for School B is\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gray]{\\dfrac{2}{3}}\n$$\n\n$\\quad$ Therefore, School A has a $\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid gray]{\\textrm{smaller}}$ fraction of instructors relative to students compared to School B." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073564 1069 "e-2265" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073564 1071 "What is missing in the following sentence?\n\n> To make a fertilizer Esther mixed $2$ quarts of fertilizer concentrate and $3$ quarts of distilled water, so there are $0$ quarts of fertilizer concentrate for each quart of distilled water." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073564 1072 #uuid "44e1e2da-6e2f-50b1-b982-dd5d4b5a432c" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073564 1074 "The ratio of the number of quarts of fertilizer concentrate to the number of quarts of distilled water is ${\\color{blue}{2}}:{\\color{red}{3}}$.\n\nWe can rewrite this ratio as a fraction, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}{2}}}{{\\color{red}{3}}}\n$$\n\nThis means that there is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nquart of fertilizer concentrate for each quart of distilled water." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073565 1069 "e-2267" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073565 1071 "From left to right, what are the missing numbers in the following sentence?\n\n> A chef requires $\\dfrac{1}{7}$ grams of chilies for each gram of turmeric for his recipe. Therefore, the recipe has a ratio of $0$ grams of chilies to $0$ grams of turmeric." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073565 1072 #uuid "cd725418-654f-5cb2-a77a-9f6f9ed27825" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073565 1074 "Let's rewrite the fraction\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{7}\n$$\n\nas a ratio:\n\n$$\n1:7\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recipe has a ratio of $1$ gram of chilies to $7$ grams of turmeric." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073566 1069 "e-2269" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073566 1071 "In two classrooms, Classroom A has $32$ students and $8$ laptops, while Classroom B has $18$ students and $6$ laptops. Fill in the missing details in the explanations below.\n\n$\\quad$ The number of laptops relative to students for Classroom A is $\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid gray]{\\phantom{1/4}}$.\n\n$\\quad$ The number of laptops relative to students for Classroom B is $\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid gray]{\\phantom{1/3}}$.\n\n$\\quad$ Therefore, Classroom A has a $\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid gray]{\\phantom{\\textrm{larger}}}$ fraction of laptops relative to students compared to Classroom B." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073566 1072 #uuid "202c3bde-e0c5-5878-b8e5-b6a3d7f7280a" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073566 1074 "The number of laptops relative to students for Classroom A is the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{32}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing both the numerator and the denominator by $8{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8 \\div 8}{32 \\div 8} = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nThe number of laptops relative to students for Classroom B is the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{18}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing both the numerator and the denominator by $6{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6 \\div 6}{18 \\div 6} = \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTo compare\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nwe look at the denominators. Since $4$ is larger than $3$, the fraction\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nrepresents a smaller portion of the whole.\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} < \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nthe fraction for Classroom A is *smaller.*\n\nThe complete statements are as follows:\n\n$\\quad$ The number of laptops relative to students for Classroom A is\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid gray]{\\dfrac{1}{4}}\n$$\n\n$\\quad$ The number of laptops relative to students for Classroom B is\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid gray]{\\dfrac{1}{3}}\n$$\n\n$\\quad$ Therefore, Classroom A has a $\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid gray]{\\textrm{smaller}}$ fraction of laptops relative to students compared to Classroom B." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073567 1069 "e-2270" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073567 1071 "In two science clubs, Club A has $4$ mentors and $20$ students, while Club B has $6$ mentors and $18$ students. Fill in the missing details in the explanations below.\n\n$\\quad$ The ratio of students to mentors in Club A is $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gray]{\\phantom{5:1}}$.\n\n$\\quad$ The ratio of students to mentors in Club B is $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gray]{\\phantom{3:1}}$.\n\n$\\quad$ Therefore, Club A has a $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gray]{\\phantom{\\textrm{smaller}}}$ ratio of students to mentors compared to Club B." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073567 1072 #uuid "13eb41f5-e4fa-509e-8670-c49aa3564dde" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073567 1074 "The ratio of students to mentors in Club A is $20:4$. We can write this ratio as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{20}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing both the numerator and the denominator by $4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{20 \\div 4}{4 \\div 4} = \\dfrac{5}{1} = 5\n$$\n\nThis is equivalent to the ratio ${\\color{blue}5:1}$.\n\nThe ratio of students to mentors in Club B is $18:6$. We can write this ratio as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{18}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing both the numerator and the denominator by $6{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{18 \\div 6}{6 \\div 6} = \\dfrac{3}{1} = 3\n$$\n\nThis is equivalent to the ratio ${\\color{blue}3:1}$.\n\nSince $5 > 3$, the ratio of students to mentors in Club A is *larger.*\n\nHowever, because the question asks which club has the *smaller* ratio of students to mentors, Club B has fewer students per mentor.\n\nTherefore, Club A has the *larger* ratio of students to mentors.\n\nThe complete statements are as follows:\n\n$\\quad$ The ratio of students to mentors in Club A is $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gray]{5:1}$.\n\n$\\quad$ The ratio of students to mentors in Club B is $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gray]{3:1}$.\n\n$\\quad$ Therefore, Club A has a $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gray]{\\textrm{larger}}$ ratio of students to mentors compared to Club B." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073568 1069 "e-2271" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073568 1071 "At two construction sites, Site A uses $24$ bags of gravel and $15$ bags of cement, while Site B uses $18$ bags of gravel and $8$ bags of cement. Fill in the missing details in the explanation below.\n\n$\\quad$ The amount of cement relative to gravel for Site A is $\\,\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid gray]{\\phantom{0/0}}$.\n\n$\\quad$ The amount of cement relative to gravel for Site B is $\\,\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid gray]{\\phantom{0/0}}$.\n\n$\\quad$ Therefore, Site A has a $\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid gray]{\\phantom{\\textrm{larger}}}$ fraction of cement relative to gravel compared to Site B." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073568 1072 #uuid "a21d401a-6e43-55aa-be7d-7cd1c8da0187" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073568 1074 "The amount of cement relative to gravel for Site A is the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{15}{24}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing both the numerator and the denominator by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{15 \\div 3}{24 \\div 3} &= \\frac{5}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe amount of cement relative to gravel for Site B is the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{18}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing both the numerator and the denominator by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{8 \\div 2}{18 \\div 2} &= \\frac{4}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compare\n\n$$\n\\dfrac58\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac49\n$$\n\nwe can compare both to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\dfrac58 > \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac49 < \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nwe conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac58 > \\dfrac49\n$$\n\nSo, the fraction for Site A is *larger*.\n\nThe complete statements are as follows:\n\n$\\quad$ The amount of cement relative to gravel for Site A is\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid gray]{\\dfrac58}\n$$\n\n$\\quad$ The amount of cement relative to gravel for Site B is\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gray]{\\dfrac49}\n$$\n\n$\\quad$ Therefore, Site A has a $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gray]{\\textrm{larger}}$ fraction of cement relative to gravel compared to Site B." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073569 1069 "e-2277" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073569 1071 "In Jennifer's garden, there are $60$ roses. The ratio of roses to orchids is $\\dfrac{3}{5}$. How many orchids are there in the garden?" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073569 1072 #uuid "913c1995-fe26-5359-8298-e3b8379e39d8" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073569 1074 "The ratio of roses to orchids is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of roses and the denominator represents the number of orchids.\n\nWe can make a numerator of $60$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $20$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}\\times 20}{{\\color{red}5}\\times 20} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}60}}{{\\color{red}100}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in Jennifer's garden, there are $100$ orchids." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073570 1069 "e-2278" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073570 1071 "The ratio of sand to crushed stone in concrete is $\\dfrac{3}{2}$. If Daniel has $80$ kilograms of crushed stone available to make concrete, what amount of sand should he use?" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073570 1072 #uuid "2eeba279-cd91-55c7-b423-a14edb255ebb" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073570 1074 "We are given that the ratio of sand to crushed stone in concrete is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the amount of sand and the denominator represents the amount of crushed stone.\n\nWe can make a denominator of $80$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $40$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}\\times 40}{{\\color{red}2}\\times 40} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}120}}{{\\color{red}80}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $80$ kilograms of crushed stone, Daniel must use $120$ kilograms of sand." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073571 1069 "e-2285" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073571 1071 "By finding the missing values in the ratio table below, determine the graph that represents the given relationship.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $4$ | $2$ |\n| $8$ |  |\n|  | $6$ |" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073571 1072 #uuid "9a740a80-f655-5d20-a839-d9a809d4c6a4" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073571 1074 "We start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}4}:{\\color{red}2}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}4}}{{\\color{red}2}}\n$$\n\nWe can generate equivalent ratios by finding equivalent fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{4 \\cdot 2}{2 \\cdot 2} &= \\frac{8}{4} \\\\\n\\frac{4 \\cdot 3}{2 \\cdot 3} &= \\frac{12}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our ratio table:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $4$ | $2$ |\n| $8$ | $4$ |\n| $12$ | $6$ |\n\nSo, we have the following ordered pairs: $(4,2)$, $(8,4)$, $(12,6)$.\n\nPlotting these points on the coordinate plane and connecting them with a straight line, we get the following graph:\n\n![](images/8d/8def1b37d62f91a16109db0da3287042fe5893b97bf447e6ee16946b3f8d4bfc.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073572 1069 "e-2287" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073572 1071 "![](images/73/73c3b1e1e419590794caa7614bfc17c686aaae35e84fbb889ac1208a0f3d0ea3.png)\n\nWhat $x:y$ ratio is shown in the graph above?" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073572 1072 #uuid "ce72406a-a57e-53b5-b066-f147e197d317" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073572 1074 "We see that the point $(32,20)$ lies on the graph. Let's write the ratio $32:20$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{32}{20}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing both the numerator and the denominator by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{32 \\div 4}{20 \\div 4} &= \\frac{8}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the graph shows the ratio\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{5}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073573 1069 "e-2288" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073573 1071 "The tape diagram below shows the ratio between small and big tokens in a desk game. What is the ratio of big tokens to all tokens?\n\n![](images/5b/5b779b66fb6e6717e102398153f09f3cb70582a4333a68ad2aeeb97fced47c9b.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073573 1072 #uuid "032ead48-0a3f-5a5e-b604-2a2fc0d91770" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073573 1074 "The tape diagram for big tokens shows $4$ boxes. So, there are $4$ big tokens.\n\nAltogether, there are\n\n$$\n12+4=16\n$$\n\nboxes. So, there are $16$ tokens in all.\n\nTherefore, the ratio of big tokens to all tokens is $4:16$." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073574 1069 "e-2289" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073574 1071 "![](images/33/33a587df3757e82def04bbf4130e0ef681f0902ffc3086dcb6b8882c297f35ad.png)\n\nBetty is making pizza. The amount of water she needs for a given amount of flour is shown in the graph above. In its simplest form, what is the ratio of flour to water?" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073574 1072 #uuid "a0c6e401-4364-575d-94d2-97108b688d43" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073574 1074 "One of the points belonging to the graph is $(15,6)$.\n\nLet's write the ratio $15:6$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{15}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing both the numerator and the denominator by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{15 \\div 3}{6 \\div 3} &= \\frac{5}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the ratio of flour to water is $5:2$." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073575 1069 "e-2290" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073575 1071 "Abbie and Kate are making cupcakes. Abbie makes $5$ cupcakes for every $7$ that Kate makes. Which tape diagram represents the given scenario?" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073575 1072 #uuid "fe68a823-4477-5d82-bd17-12842d6e8735" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073575 1074 "We are told that:\n\n> Abbie makes $\\color{blue}5$ cupcakes for every $\\color{red}7$ that Kate makes.\n\nSo, in a tape diagram, Abbie's tape must contain $\\color{blue}5$ boxes, while Kate's tape should have $\\color{red}7$ boxes.\n\nTherefore, the diagram is the following:\n\n![](images/47/47c6e186cd17bffc7e83cca0c228bc1d05287cfba1bdb394c0a5dd71d411294a.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073576 1069 "e-2291" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073576 1071 "What is the missing number in the statement below?\n\n$\\qquad$ $16$ chickens in $2$ chicken coops $0$ chickens per chicken coop." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073576 1072 #uuid "579bda4d-058c-5572-8d5d-addf9650d1ed" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073576 1074 "The ratio of the number of chickens to the number of chicken coops is ${\\color{blue}16}:{\\color{red}2}$. So we start with the ratio $16:2$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}16}}{{\\color{red}2}}\n$$\n\nWe can make a denominator of $1$ by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{16 \\div 2}{2 \\div 2} = \\dfrac{8}{1} = 8\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$\\qquad$ $16$ chickens in $2$ chicken coops $= 8$ chickens per chicken coop." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073577 1069 "e-2292" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073577 1071 "Carl walks $450$ meters in $5$ minutes. If he walks at a constant pace, at what rate does he walk?" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073577 1072 #uuid "199c8a83-265f-598b-a6f3-a606f3bbd7a7" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073577 1074 "The ratio of meters to minutes that Carl walks is ${\\color{blue}450}:{\\color{red}5}$. So we start with the ratio $450:5$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}450}}{{\\color{red}5}}\n$$\n\nWe can make a denominator of $1$ by dividing the numerator and denominator by $5$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}450}\\div 5}{{\\color{red}5}\\div 5} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}90}}{{\\color{red}1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Carl walks at a rate of $90$ meters per minute." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073578 1069 "e-2293" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073578 1071 "Anthony says $400$ words in $6$ minutes. If he speaks at a steady pace, what statement describes the unit rate?" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073578 1072 #uuid "3997c678-94c6-5f9f-bf6a-dc752e89b3fe" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073578 1074 "The ratio of spoken words to minutes is ${\\color{blue}400}:{\\color{red}6}$. So we start with the ratio $400:6$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}400}}{{\\color{red}6}}\n$$\n\nWe can reduce this fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{400 \\div 2}{6 \\div 2} = \\dfrac{200}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Anthony said\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{200}{3}\n$$\n\nwords per minute." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073579 1069 "e-2294" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073579 1071 "Donald writes $3$ words in $2$ seconds. If he writes at a steady pace, how many words does he write per second?" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073579 1072 #uuid "ffe79c28-3eef-51a9-9185-283676fdaf65" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073579 1074 "The ratio of words to seconds is ${\\color{blue}3}:{\\color{red}2}$. Expressing this as a fraction, we have:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}}{{\\color{red}2}}\n$$\n\nWe can convert this fraction to a decimal by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3 \\times 5}{2 \\times 5} = \\dfrac{15}{10} = 1.5\n$$\n\nTherefore, Donald writes $1.5$ words per second." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073580 1069 "e-2295" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073580 1071 "The tape diagram below shows the ratio between the number of orange plastic building blocks and blue plastic building blocks that Tom has. If there are $54$ orange plastic building blocks, how many plastic building blocks are there altogether?\n\n![](images/8c/8c05c3e85b33f385b2f4be02d57d1d34fdfcd94a6ca61b44411859d188f3d2cc.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073580 1072 #uuid "c089129a-f06b-53d3-a7be-d8d8a79b6dd9" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073580 1074 "We are told that there are $54$ orange plastic building blocks. The tape diagram for orange plastic building blocks shows $6$ boxes. Therefore, each box in the diagram above represents\n\n$$\n54 \\div 6 = 9\n$$\n\nplastic building blocks.\n\nNow, we fill the number $9$ in each box of both tapes:\n\n![](images/16/16c543fb48a3d3c89c36b969cd4fceec36dd0c880664fd979c76740fd12c5722.png)\n\nSince there are $10$ boxes corresponding to the plastic building blocks in total, and each box represents $9$ plastic building blocks, the total number of plastic building blocks is:\n\n$$\n10 \\times 9 = 90\n$$\n\nSo, there are $90$ plastic building blocks." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073581 1069 "e-2296" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073581 1071 "A jar contains $60$ raffle tickets. The ratio of winning tickets to nonwinning tickets is shown in the diagram above. How many nonwinning tickets are in the jar?\n\n![](images/8b/8bdce5e82b4bcda74730503edebb9816d66175a1324784b11b9b08eeb17c9ba9.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073581 1072 #uuid "ca5d9154-4c7e-5866-a6eb-5e4c1fb4d24b" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073581 1074 "The tape diagram shows $1$ box corresponding to winning tickets, and $4$ boxes corresponding to nonwinning tickets. Therefore, the ratio of winning to nonwinning tickets is $1:4$.\n\nWe are told that there are $60$ tickets in total. The tape diagram has\n\n$$\n1+4=5\n$$\n\nboxes in total. Therefore, each box in the diagram above represents\n\n$$\n60 \\div 5 = 12\n$$\n\ntickets.\n\nNow, we fill the number $12$ in each box of both tapes:\n\n![](images/16/1669ac0d1aa6edd5bf53609121080dc8e8b685b57daa60b91daf3db816474ef2.png)\n\nSince there are $4$ boxes corresponding to nonwinning tickets, and each box represents $12$ tickets, the number of nonwinning tickets must be:\n\n$$\n4 \\times 12 = 48\n$$\n\nSo, there are $48$ nonwinning tickets in the jar." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073582 1069 "e-2308" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073582 1071 "Which of the following models shows $38\\%$?\n\n![](images/94/9424e7c21944e0953f1611e73b05a4e2d7c1c654cea34b6a2ed96e92e2fbfdd9.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073582 1072 #uuid "77ca02d3-234c-512d-bd8f-7a8d3ca111f7" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073582 1074 "Recall that $38\\%$ is the same as $38$ out of $100$.\n\nSo, we need a model that shows $38$ shaded tiles out of $100$. The correct model is:\n\n![](images/b6/b6d817845f75872a31e275f2ad77021a6a7cc7917e6aeed2b5ac060c6c24a441.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073583 1069 "e-2312" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073583 1071 "Given that a large square represents a whole, draw a model that shows $130\\%$." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073583 1072 #uuid "4d698fe6-f5af-5915-8b63-152d67c52774" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073583 1074 "First, recall that $130\\%$ is the same as $130$ out of $100$.\n\nSo, we need a model where $100$ tiles represent one whole, and $130$ tiles are shaded. This model is as follows:\n\n![](images/9b/9b103762f22198b9cbb6ac5afcd4c39b9ce99e4afee6ebac12b24a214698bed0.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073584 1069 "e-2321" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073584 1071 "Which of the models below represents $25\\%$?\n\n![](images/c0/c01ebc79f3a5ce07673069f16cde018856f7e3281dc20feb4e06d7ccffbfe4f6.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073584 1072 #uuid "62f2309e-804b-5ccc-9dbc-c8c6cc1a04b3" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073584 1074 "First, recall that $25\\%$ is the same as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{25}{100}\n$$\n\nWe now need to simplify this fraction. Dividing the numerator and denominator by $5$, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{25}{100} = \\dfrac{25\\div 5}{100\\div 5} = \\dfrac{5}{20}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide the numerator and denominator by $5$ once more:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{20} = \\dfrac{5\\div 5}{20\\div 5} = \\dfrac14\n$$\n\nThe model that shows\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nis:\n\n![](images/05/05a7e7f341e26def763ad0f29169d72c152bd479361eec4aea6d2d5880a88ab7.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073585 1069 "e-2322" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073585 1071 "If a $10$-sided polygon represents a whole, draw a model representing $110\\%$." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073585 1072 #uuid "152c00bd-bae3-5abf-b461-0c031b4fb462" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073585 1074 "First, recall that $110\\%$ is the same as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{110}{100}\n$$\n\nNow, we simplify the fraction. Dividing the numerator and denominator by $10$, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{110}{100} = \\dfrac{110 \\div 10}{100 \\div 10} = \\dfrac{11}{10}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{11}{10}\n$$\n\nas a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{11}{10} = 1 \\, \\text{R} \\, 1 = 1 \\, \\dfrac{1}{10}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our model could be the following:\n\n![](images/c9/c951b3f3f45c7d502d447d5371286f095b060cade366c01723ea3118ee55da4a.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073586 1069 "e-2526" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073586 1071 "The graph below represents the relationship $0$ What is the missing number?\n\n![](images/b3/b3548f5a4d74c8241f58ea9caa0ca240baddbfd5cfb1dd54b596fc4c32e70d11.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073586 1072 #uuid "68acf530-39da-5219-b6a5-dd9eff9e55ff" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073586 1074 "From the graph, we can pick out the following coordinates:\n$(2,1),\\: (4,2),\\: (6,3),\\: (8,4)$\n\nNow let's put these coordinates into the table:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $2$ | $1$ |\n| $4$ | $2$ |\n| $6$ | $3$ |\n| $8$ | $4$ |\n\nNotice that for every $x$-value, we divide by $2$ to get the corresponding $y$-value.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $2$ | $\\dfrac2{\\color{blue}2} = 1$ |\n| $4$ | $\\dfrac4{\\color{blue}2} = 2$ |\n| $6$ | $\\dfrac6{\\color{blue}2} = 3$ |\n| $8$ | $\\dfrac8{\\color{blue}2} = 4$ |\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{x}{\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\color{blue}2}}\n$$\n\nSo the missing number is ${\\color{blue}{2}}$." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073587 1069 "e-2527" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073587 1071 "Sketch the graph that represents the equation $y = x + 3$." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073587 1072 #uuid "711f9305-7f0c-5121-a071-b96182308b7d" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073587 1074 "First, we create a table representing the equation\n\n$$\ny = x + {\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nFor some chosen values of $x$, we evaluate $y{:}$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0+{\\color{blue}3} = 3$ |\n| $1$ | $1+{\\color{blue}3} = 4$ |\n| $2$ | $2+{\\color{blue}3} = 5$ |\n| $3$ | $3+{\\color{blue}3} = 6$ |\n| $4$ | $4+{\\color{blue}3} = 7$ |\n\nNow, we plot these points and connect them with a line. When we do this, we get the following plot.\n\n![](images/4b/4ba64522cfdee4412cddd08465390c256b2255d2a721d75a20e7e9357def4c00.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073588 1069 "e-2528" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073588 1071 "The graph shows the number of hotcakes $h$ that Esther can make with $e$ eggs. How many eggs does Esther need to make $15$ hotcakes?\n\n![](images/00/00fa5f4711f1a0d30eb63a47559114042ac5e74430c7c00d5ed7731a2689c16b.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073588 1072 #uuid "15a45c17-680c-5f33-aac1-a555760f70ae" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073588 1074 "Since Esther wants to make $15$ hotcakes, we have that $h = 15$.\n\nWe need to locate the $e$ value that corresponds to $h = 15$ on the graph. To do this, we:\n\n- find $h = {\\color{blue}15}$ on the vertical axis,\n- move horizontally from this point until we reach the graph, and\n- move vertically down from the graph and read off the $e$-value.\n\n![](images/eb/eb74a91de010ecac9404a0b25de3dc8039dff84b172b8d06d4eeb0f66a266070.png)\n\nThe corresponding value on the horizontal axis is\n\n$$\ne = {\\color{red}5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Esther needs $5$ eggs to make $15$ hotcakes." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073589 1069 "e-2530" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073589 1071 "Moises charges his laptop. The percentage charge $p$ of the laptop's battery after $t$ minutes is shown in the graph. What will be the battery's charge percentage after $23$ minutes?\n\n![](images/ba/bacde03d2e218405e522952758fd35f2402503ef8bc576489053c37472604a9a.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073589 1072 #uuid "f92099f5-2139-532b-9818-a069ff59ab17" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073589 1074 "Since the laptop charges for $23$ minutes, we have that $t=23$.\n\nOn the graph, we can't identify a point corresponding to $t=23$, so we will need to find the equation relating $p$ and $t$.\n\nFrom the graph, we can pick out the following coordinates:\n$(5,10),\\: (10,20),\\: (15,30)$\n\nNow, let's put these coordinates into a table:\n\n| $t$ | $p$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $5$ | $10$ |\n| $10$ | $20$ |\n| $15$ | $30$ |\n\nNotice that for every $t$-value, we multiply by $2$ to get the corresponding $p$-value.\n\n| $t$ | $p$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $5$ | ${\\color{blue}2}\\cdot5=10$ |\n| $10$ | ${\\color{blue}2}\\cdot10=20$ |\n| $15$ | ${\\color{blue}2}\\cdot15=30$ |\n\nHence, the equation that relates $p$ and $t$ is\n\n$$\np={\\color{blue}2}t\n$$\n\nSo, for $t=23$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np &= 2 \\cdot 23 = 46\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $23$ minutes, the charge level will be $46\\%$." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073590 1069 "e-2609" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073590 1071 "Which of the following diagrams represents a perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}$?\n\n![](images/90/90e49cf78c7fb86b6f5a8c33b3e5bd28279248f791757a2351ddb86296d79d72.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073590 1072 #uuid "d49eb5a2-baaf-527f-bfa7-12b2befa865a" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073590 1074 "A perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}$:\n\n- must split $\\overline{AB}$ in half, and\n- must be perpendicular to $\\overline{AB}$.\n\nAmong the given options, only diagram IV shows a perpendicular bisector to the line segment $\\overline{AB}$.\n\nNotice that in diagrams I, II and III we have bisectors but they are not perpendicular to $\\overline{AB}$." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073591 1069 "e-2610" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073591 1071 "Solve for $x$ in the figure below given that $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}$.\n\n![](images/2d/2dea948c7ab1cf3bb7f543c651d98f23cda8531bbec8cc44e12736c3f04f04c3.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073591 1072 #uuid "01b53ce1-80d9-530a-bfba-cfd500b37f9d" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073591 1074 "By the perpendicular bisector theorem, a point on the perpendicular bisector of a segment is equidistant from the endpoints of the segment. Hence, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nDA &= DB \\\\\nx + 5 &= 2x \\\\\nx &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073592 1069 "e-2613" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073592 1071 "Solve for $x$ in the figure below given that $AD=BD$ and $AE=BE$.\n\n![](images/e5/e58ea5aadf9075a8b1d7a3de3a3c82e88eb3b47281cf75134631d3a153d42127.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073592 1072 #uuid "ab4fe8cf-3651-5a1f-a796-546892cb0178" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073592 1074 "By the converse of the perpendicular bisector theorem, a point equidistant from the endpoints of a segment lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment.\n\nHere, the points $D$ and $E$ are both equidistant from the endpoints of $\\overline{AB}$. Therefore, $D$ and $E$ both lie on the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}$.\n\nSince only one line can be drawn through two given points, the line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{DE}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}$.\n\nIn particular, we have that $AC=BC$. We can use this equation to solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAC &= BC \\\\\n3x + 2 &= 5x - 2 \\\\\n4 &= 2x \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073593 1069 "e-2647" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073593 1071 "What is the distance between the points $(2,4)$ and $(5,4)$?\n\n![](images/ce/ce70a8122559f67aac379349fb369e6e7e007e85fa8a13632e7865469804fb6a.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073593 1072 #uuid "fb5d2269-5350-5d79-9414-1a25caa48d8c" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073593 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $3$ units, so the distance equals $3$.\n\n![](images/bd/bd568e789e79574225dbd75e79d4cacb2fbf92beb048f5bdafeb67f74ab029f6.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $y$-axis and then subtracting.\n\n![](images/fb/fb2f075f8321aca3823770e28a86a8d9532cb343ba4239f94c2fb9d1cc883b0b.png)\n\nThe point $({\\color{blue}{5}},4)$ is at a distance of $\\color{blue}{5}$ from the $y$-axis, and the point $({\\color{red}{2}},4)$ is at a distance of $\\color{red}{2}$ from the $y$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{5}} - {\\color{red}{2}} = 3\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073594 1069 "e-2648" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073594 1071 "What is the distance between the points $(-4,-2)$ and $(-4,-5)$?" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073594 1072 #uuid "1250785a-1ae3-5218-925a-0eac01281f3f" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073594 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nWe plot the given points in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/0d/0d26dd64fad1809f58fecdf802a28b5ab6d1dd201b63dc0bb9293f248a127a85.png)\n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $3$ units, so the distance equals $3$.\n\n![](images/fb/fbc52312edf77508a9d7436acd515bc91e3225826c16b85c3571143c39886771.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $x$-axis and then subtracting.\n\n![](images/80/80be7ec0ed67ce819b51b34f7a50aeb51786e3a6ca9190b418b2ec96e127cc78.png)\n\nThe point $(-4,{\\color{red}{-5}})$ is at a distance of $\\color{red}{5}$ from the $x$-axis, and the point $(-4,{\\color{blue}{-2}})$ is at a distance of $\\color{blue}{2}$ from the $x$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{5}} - {\\color{blue}{2}} = 3\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073595 1069 "e-2649" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073595 1071 "What is the distance between the points $(3,4)$ and $(3,-3)$?" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073595 1072 #uuid "e05457af-60c5-519f-935e-db465fc76efa" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073595 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nWe plot the given points in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/47/47f203755bf0abcbf33be636331e4f9c0119c14620f1a658be31db0d6feeed17.png)\n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $7$ units, so the distance equals $7$.\n\n![](images/79/79b944cc0d1af7577282653e3250af844ad10256bb3b6cf2b204ed2f9862d480.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $x$-axis and then adding.\n\n![](images/94/94a5e438b160ce6f3dca0f44fd418a8831c520543e7dad605ea23674500af5fd.png)\n\nThe point $(3,{\\color{blue}{4}})$ is at a distance of $\\color{blue}{4}$ from the $x$-axis, and the point $(3,{\\color{red}{-3}})$ is at a distance of $\\color{red}{3}$ from the $x$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{3}} + {\\color{blue}{4}} = 7\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073596 1069 "e-2888" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073596 1071 "The point $Q(4,3)$ is shifted $2$ units down, reflected across the $y$-axis, and then shifted $1$ unit to the right. What is the resulting point?" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073596 1072 #uuid "6b45326c-ce13-5d2f-84df-b3698b46ea68" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073596 1074 "Let's recall the functional representations of our three transformations:\n\n- A shift of $2$ units down can be represented by the function\n$(x,y) \\mapsto (x,y-2)$.\n- A reflection in the $y$-axis can be represented by the function\n$(x,y) \\mapsto (-x,y)$.\n\n- A shift of $1$ unit to the right can be represented by the function\n$(x,y) \\mapsto (x+1,y)$.\n\nLet's apply the given transformations to the point $Q$ using our functions:\n\nFirst, we apply the shift. The image of the point\n\n$$\n\\left(4,3\\right)\n$$\n\nunder the shift is\n\n$$\n(4,3) \\mapsto (4,3-2)=(4, 1)\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the reflection. The image of the point\n\n$$\n\\left(4,1\\right)\n$$\n\nunder the reflection is\n\n$$\n(4, 1) \\mapsto(-4,1)\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the second shift. The image of the point\n\n$$\n\\left(-4,1\\right)\n$$\n\nunder the shift is\n\n$$\n(-4,1) \\mapsto (-4+1,1)=(-3, 1)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resulting point is $(-3,1)$." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073597 1069 "e-2893" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073597 1071 "The point $P(5,14)$ is reflected across the line $y=x$ and then stretched according to the function $(x,y) \\mapsto (x,ky)$. If the image of $P$ under this sequence of transformations is $(14,25)$, then what is the value of $k$?" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073597 1072 #uuid "b45dae26-7996-5544-9884-d604bac62993" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073597 1074 "Let's apply the given transformations.\n\nA reflection across the line $y=x$ can be represented by\n$(x,y) \\mapsto (y, x)$.\nApplying this to the point $P$, we get\n$(5,14) \\mapsto (14,5)$.\n\nNow, applying the stretch to $(14,5)$, we get\n$(14, 5) \\mapsto (14, 5k)$.\n\nSince the resulting point is $(14,25)$, by equating the $y$-coordinates, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5k = 25⟹k &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=5$." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073598 1069 "e-2914" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073598 1071 "According to the SSS criterion only, which of the following triangles is congruent to $\\mathcal{T}$?\n\n![](images/af/af83d12851a76b59cb22261c13bb4688825cdb1ebb8caf7ebb5f0b4c4444b01d.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073598 1072 #uuid "38b3c2b4-9373-5f3b-8a48-cf2fa8f17346" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073598 1074 "The SSS (side-side-side) congruence criterion states the following:\n\n> Two triangles are congruent if and only if three sides of one triangle are equal to three sides of the other triangle.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given triangles.\n\n- $\\mathcal{P}$ is congruent to $\\mathcal{T}$ by SSS since all the three sides of these two triangles are equal.\n- $\\mathcal{Q}$ is *not* congruent to $\\mathcal{T}$ by SSS since we have only two pairs of congruent sides.\n- $\\mathcal{S}$ is *not* congruent to $\\mathcal{T}$ by SSS since we have only one pair of congruent sides.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\mathcal{P}$ only.\"" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073599 1069 "e-2916" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073599 1071 "According to the SSS criterion only, which of the following triangles is congruent to $\\triangle OBC$?\n\n![](images/5c/5c2e823d6f54e9c635fdfe6d11501fa51a769b362172791f602234222a7ab7e4.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073599 1072 #uuid "cf9384bd-2298-5045-b215-14906fd5f843" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073599 1074 "From the diagram, $\\triangle OBC$ and $\\triangle OBA$ are congruent by SSS (side-side-side).\n\nIndeed, $\\overline{OB}$ is the common side of the triangles, and we have the following additional congruent pairs of sides:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}AB^(―) & ≅BC^(―) \\\\ OA^(―) & ≅OC^(―)\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073600 1069 "e-2918" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073600 1071 "In the figure below, $\\overline{SR} \\cong \\overline{QP}$ and $\\overline{SP} \\cong \\overline{QR}$. If $m \\angle RSP = 67^\\circ$ and $m \\angle PQR = 2x+12^\\circ$, find the value of $x$.\n\n![](images/9c/9c39d004ea14e1fe9e4351b08010a8d06d8f2192a2470604b3199816d7301af2.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073600 1072 #uuid "69b5fe36-0606-58d4-85de-02441112033d" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073600 1074 "From the diagram, $\\triangle RSP$ and $\\triangle PQR$ are congruent by SSS (side-side-side) since $\\overline{PR}$ is their common side and we have two more pairs of congruent sides:\n\n$$\n\\overline{SR} \\cong \\overline{PQ}\\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad\\overline{SP} \\cong \\overline{QR}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\angle RSP \\cong \\angle PQR\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠RSP &= m∠PQR \\\\\n67^{∘} &= 2x + 12^{∘} \\\\\n2x &= 55^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 27.5^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073601 1069 "e-2919" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073601 1071 "According to the ASA criterion only, which of the following triangles are congruent to $\\mathcal{P}$?\n\n![](images/18/1845fa30b2716c55494147541ecf2d2fbe6795035c293a5bef343307c6c5f3bf.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073601 1072 #uuid "02de7f94-4040-5e58-a93b-bcc81c20386a" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073601 1074 "The ASA (angle-side-angle) congruence criterion states the following:\n\n> Two triangles are congruent if and only if two angles and the included side of one triangle are congruent to two angles and the included side of the other triangle.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given triangles.\n\n- $\\mathcal{P}$ is *not* congruent to $\\mathcal{T}_1$ by ASA since they don't have congruent angles.\n- $\\mathcal{P}$ is *not* congruent to $\\mathcal{T}_2$ by ASA since they don't have congruent sides.\n- $\\mathcal{P} \\cong \\mathcal{T}_3$ by ASA since two angles and the included side of one triangle are congruent to two angles and the included side of the other triangle.\n\nTherefore, only $\\mathcal{T}_3$ is congruent to $\\mathcal{P}$ by the ASA criterion." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073602 1069 "e-2921" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073602 1071 "From the diagram below, which of the following statements is true?\n\n1. $\\triangle ABC \\cong \\triangle DEF$ by ASA\n2. $x = 1$\n3. $y = 1$\n\n![](images/dd/ddc4844c21c2d1010eb2a7f6e4a72951f7bb4942f9382be0c98331030908f11d.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073602 1072 #uuid "1ae2d912-7d01-5113-9e0c-6c4cc0d8dbbe" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073602 1074 "Let's examine each of the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle DEF$ are congruent by ASA since we have the following pairs of congruent angles and included sides:$\\overline{AB} \\cong \\overline{DE}$$\\angle A \\cong \\angle D$$\\angle B \\cong \\angle E$\n- Statement II is false. Since the triangles are congruent, all the corresponding sides must be congruent. In particular,\n$BC|= EF; 2x - 5|= 5; 2x|= 10; x|= 5 \\ne 1$.\n- Statement III is true. Since the triangles are congruent, all the corresponding sides must be congruent. In particular,\n$AC|= DF; 3|= 4y - 1; 4y|= 4; y|= 1$.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073603 1069 "e-2926" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073603 1071 "In the figure below, $AC=12 \\,$, $AE=5\\,$, $ED= 7\\,$, $BD=10 \\,$, and $CE = 3x-2$. What is the value of $x$?\n\n![](images/25/259362a6db8147f47e26cf3484d7ec09caba9864cd1349f165a6bef90ff7642c.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073603 1072 #uuid "2ed9ad1d-9835-5ab1-b933-344964e5a7fa" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073603 1074 "First, notice that $\\overline{AC} \\cong \\overline{AD}$ since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAD &= AE + ED \\\\\n&= 5 + 7 \\\\\n&= 12 \\\\\n&= AC\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $\\triangle CAE$ and $\\triangle DAB$ are congruent by ASA since we have the following pairs of congruent angles and included sides:\n\n- $\\overline{AC} \\cong \\overline{AD}$\n- $\\angle A$ is their common angle\n- $\\angle{C} \\cong \\angle{D}$\n\nTherefore, all the corresponding sides of the triangles must be congruent. In particular, $\\overline{CE} \\cong \\overline{BD}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nCE &= BD \\\\\n3x - 2 &= 10 \\\\\n3x &= 12 \\\\\nx &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073604 1069 "e-2968" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073604 1071 "Given that the two polygons shown below are similar, determine the similarity ratio.\n\n*Hint: Consider the ratio of the **larger** polygon to the **smaller** polygon.*\n\n![](images/f4/f4da94c11054e9e42b7dc9f6c085d3c6be5fdf026ba9d913fb7b060d56221967.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073604 1072 #uuid "da7af116-26c8-56e4-a6e5-b195cbb7699e" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073604 1074 "Given two similar polygons, the similarity ratio is the ratio of any two corresponding sides.\n\nNotice that, for example, $\\overline{LM}$ is the longest side of $NLMP$. Hence, it corresponds to the longest side of $ABCD$, which is $\\overline{BC}$.\n\nTherefore, the similarity ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk &= \\frac{BC}{LM} \\\\\n&= \\frac{33}{22} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The ratio of the smaller polygon to the larger polygon would be\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)}=\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073605 1069 "e-2969" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073605 1071 "Which of the following quadrilaterals are similar to $\\mathcal{R}$ (other than $\\mathcal R$ itself)?\n\n![](images/5a/5a7c9208a16a639ef6b08855a6caaacb39c121dd4ccf45271d98fcc70f1a8fed.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073605 1072 #uuid "fd7f22e7-9aba-5936-beb1-56d3a5e016cc" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073605 1074 "First, notice that $\\mathcal{R}$ is a rectangle, meaning all the angles measure $90^\\circ$, and it has $2$ pairs of parallel congruent sides.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given polygons.\n\n- $\\mathcal{Q}$ is *not* similar to $\\mathcal{R}$. The polygon $\\mathcal{Q}$ is a rectangle, and the corresponding angles are congruent, but the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are not the same:\n$\\dfrac{8}{6}\\neq\\dfrac{3}{3}$\n- $\\mathcal{P}$ is similar to $\\mathcal{R}$. Indeed, $\\mathcal{P}$ is a rectangle. Furthermore, the corresponding angles are congruent, and the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are the same:\n$\\dfrac{4}{6}=\\dfrac{2}{3}$\n- $\\mathcal{S}$ is *not* similar to $\\mathcal{R}$ because $\\mathcal{S}$ is not a rectangle (it's a parallelogram).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\mathcal{P}$ only.\"" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073606 1069 "e-2970" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073606 1071 "Given that the two quadrilaterals below are similar, write down a correct similarity statement.\n\n![](images/bf/bfff4195302736df01985ca9db83d373243833023003730004cd93512467e4f4.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073606 1072 #uuid "98596871-dc8e-5083-a773-1a70ffe1a159" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073606 1074 "First, let's find the measures of unknown angles. Since the sum of the measures of internal angles of a quadrilateral is $360^\\circ$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠A + m∠B + m∠C + m∠D &= 360^{∘} \\\\\n55^{∘} + m∠B + 90^{∘} + 125^{∘} &= 360^{∘} \\\\\nm∠B + 270^{∘} &= 360^{∘} \\\\\nm∠B &= 90^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠K + m∠L + m∠M + m∠N &= 360^{∘} \\\\\n55^{∘} + 90^{∘} + m∠M + 125^{∘} &= 360^{∘} \\\\\nm∠M + 270^{∘} &= 360^{∘} \\\\\nm∠M &= 90^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the quadrilaterals are similar, and\n\n$$\n∠A≅∠K, ∠D≅∠N, ∠C≅∠L≅∠B≅∠M\n$$\n\nSo far, we have that\n\n- $A$ corresponds to $K$, and\n- $D$ corresponds to $N$.\n\nFrom the angle measurements, it's unclear whether $C$ corresponds to $L$ or $M$ (these are all right angles). However, $C$ sits between a $125^\\circ$ angle and a $90^\\circ$ angle, and so does $M$ (whereas $L$ does not). So, we also have that\n\n- $C$ corresponds to $M$, and\n- $B$ corresponds to $L$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that a correct similarity statement is\n$ABCD \\sim KLMN$." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073607 1069 "e-2972" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073607 1071 "Given that $ABCD \\sim A'B'C'D'$, $AB = 3\\,\\mathrm{ft}$, and $BC = 4\\,\\mathrm{ft}$,\nwhich of the following could be the values of $A'B'$ and $B'C'$?\n\n1. ${A'B'} = 15\\, \\mathrm{ft}, \\, {B'C'} = 20\\, \\mathrm{ft}$\n2. ${A'B'} = 30\\, \\mathrm{ft}, \\, {B'C'} = 44\\, \\mathrm{ft}$\n3. ${A'B'} = 18\\, \\mathrm{ft}, \\, {B'C'} = 24\\, \\mathrm{ft}$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073607 1072 #uuid "8fda1604-bea3-5061-9a3f-a0ec86f95a41" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073607 1074 "For similar polygons, the ratios of lengths of the corresponding sides should be the same.\n\n- For option I, the ratios are the same:\n$\\frac{AB}{A^{′}B^{′}}|= \\frac{3}{15} = \\frac{1}{5}; \\frac{BC}{B^{′}C^{′}}|= \\frac{4}{20} = \\frac{1}{5}$\nSo, option I indeed gives possible values of $A'B'$ and $B'C'$.\n- For option II, the ratios are different:\n$\\frac{AB}{A^{′}B^{′}}|= \\frac{3}{30} = \\frac{1}{10}; \\frac{BC}{B^{′}C^{′}}|= \\frac{4}{44} = \\frac{1}{11}$\nSo, option II does not give possible values of $A'B'$ and $B'C'$.\n- For option III, the ratios are the same:\n$\\frac{AB}{A^{′}B^{′}}|= \\frac{3}{18} = \\frac{1}{6}; \\frac{BC}{B^{′}C^{′}}|= \\frac{4}{24} = \\frac{1}{6}$\nSo, option III indeed gives possible values of $A'B'$ and $B'C'$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073608 1069 "e-2978" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073608 1071 "![](images/44/44a9e1bab3de94acd0a1195f1bbb8b09372e651b4938981542374c2b6fb96370.png)\n\nIf the figures $\\mathcal P$ and $\\mathcal Q$ are similar, then what transformation maps $\\mathcal P$ onto $\\mathcal Q$?" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073608 1072 #uuid "54f05d2d-1137-5bc7-b56d-0215dc3895ad" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073608 1074 "The figures $\\mathcal P$ and $\\mathcal Q$ are similar. The ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are all equal, and all corresponding angles are equal.\n\nIndeed, $\\mathcal Q$ is the image of $\\mathcal P$ under a dilation with center at the origin and scale factor\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/47/47df13ba70a02c17afc5269d471e6381e8cc980ee0f89380f656ae625b761c9b.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073609 1069 "e-2993" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073609 1071 "Which of the following transformations map any polygon to a similar polygon?\n\n1. $(x,y) \\mapsto (-x, y)$\n2. $(x,y) \\mapsto (x, y-1)$\n3. $(x,y) \\mapsto (3x, 2y)$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073609 1072 #uuid "a75b57a8-4819-594a-9aa2-e35b54a68f6a" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073609 1074 "A similarity transformation is any transformation made up of rigid motions (rotations, reflections, and translations) and dilations.\n\nA similarity transformation will map any polygon to a similar polygon.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the transformations in turn.\n\n- Transformation I represents a reflection in the $y$-axis. Thus, it is a rigid motion and, therefore, a similarity transformation.\n- Transformation II represents a translation by $1$ unit downward. Thus, it is a similarity transformation.\n- Transformation III represents a stretch with different stretch factors in the $x$- and $y$ directions. This is not a similarity transformation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073610 1069 "e-3012" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073610 1071 "In the figure below, $\\overline{PQ} \\parallel \\overline{AC}$. Determine the measure of $\\overline{BP}$.\n\n![](images/c8/c841fbc3db8c287178fc849c432451156ff99bbf43d8996fefe1ee89e97fe9c0.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073610 1072 #uuid "c72e109e-52a0-50b2-9d8f-3f9f08f619a4" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073610 1074 "First, let's find $BQ{:}$\n\n$$\nBQ = AB-AQ = 35-15=20\n$$\n\nThe triangle proportionality theorem states the following:\n\n> If a line parallel to a side of a triangle intersects the other two sides, then the line divides those sides proportionally.\n\nSince ${\\color{blue}\\overline{PQ}} \\parallel \\overline{AC}$, then by the triangle proportionality theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{BP}{PC} &= \\frac{BQ}{QA} \\\\\n\\frac{BP}{12} &= \\frac{20}{15}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the above equation for $BP$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{BP}{12} &= \\frac{4}{3} \\\\\nBP &= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073611 1069 "e-3013" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073611 1071 "In the figure below, $\\overline{DE} \\parallel \\overline{BC}$, ${AE} = 3x-2$, $EC = x+6$, $AD = 12$, and $DB = 9$. Find the value of $x$.\n\n![](images/b5/b5162f71ff288c76d6762345d55dec45998bd2291af6f8ccf7f9ff5bccbdff88.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073611 1072 #uuid "456c2239-6f12-57a7-88f5-1ecea6ebf6fd" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073611 1074 "The triangle proportionality theorem states the following:\n\n> If a line parallel to a side of a triangle intersects the other two sides, then the line divides those sides proportionally.\n\nSince $\\overline{DE} \\parallel \\overline{BC}$, then by the triangle proportionality theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{AE}{EC} &= \\frac{AD}{DB} \\\\\n\\frac{3x - 2}{x + 6} &= \\frac{12}{9} \\\\\n9(3x - 2) &= 12(x + 6) \\\\\n27x - 18 &= 12x + 72 \\\\\n15x &= 90 \\\\\nx &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073612 1069 "e-3014" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073612 1071 "In the triangle $\\triangle ABC$ shown below, $BD+BE=15$ and $\\overline{DE}\\parallel\\overline{AC}$. Find the length of $\\overline{BC}$.\n\n![](images/89/89e54698e9b66f88528d08e5c71845698bfde3f42bdf8023b3d9fa3b848e1967.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073612 1072 #uuid "702c0816-236e-5ddd-9902-e8aef0f40df8" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073612 1074 "The triangle proportionality theorem states the following:\n\n> If a line parallel to a side of a triangle intersects the other two sides, then the line divides those sides proportionally.\n\nFrom the above diagram, we notice that $BD=6$. Now, as\n\n$$\nBD+BE=15\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\nBE=15-6=9\n$$\n\nSince $\\overline{DE}\\parallel\\overline{AC}$, then by the triangle proportionality theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{BE}{EC} &= \\frac{BD}{DA} \\\\\n\\frac{9}{EC} &= \\frac{6}{4} \\\\\n\\frac{9}{EC} &= \\frac{3}{2} \\\\\nEC &= \\frac{9 \\cdot 2}{3} \\\\\nEC &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nBC &= BE + EC \\\\\n&= 9 + 6 \\\\\n&= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073613 1069 "e-3015" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073613 1071 "In the figure below, $\\overline{AB} \\parallel \\overline{EF}$, $\\overline{BC} \\parallel \\overline{DE}$, $AD = 4$, $AB = 6$, and $BF = 2$.\nFind the measure of $\\overline{CF}$.\n\n![](images/5b/5b445bd5f7bd44956d499b18c0aa29842c77879a301ec46c8d1a49dcb4c2fe34.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073613 1072 #uuid "715caf63-b974-519f-b0ff-b8fe9a8eb591" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073613 1074 "The triangle proportionality theorem states the following:\n\n> If a line parallel to a side of a triangle intersects the other two sides, then the line divides those sides proportionally.\n\nFirst, since $AD = 4$ and\n\n$$\nAD+DB=AB = 6\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\nDB = AB-AD = 6-4=2\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the triangle proportionality theorem twice:\n\n- Since $\\overline{DE} \\parallel \\overline{BC}$, then by the triangle proportionality theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{AE}{EC} &= \\frac{AD}{DB} \\\\\n\\frac{AE}{EC} &= \\frac{4}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{AE}{EC} &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Since $\\overline{EF} \\parallel \\overline{AB}$, then by the triangle proportionality theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{CF}{FB} &= \\frac{CE}{EA} \\\\\n\\frac{CF}{FB} &= (\\frac{AE}{EC})^{-1} \\\\\n\\frac{CF}{2} &= 2^{-1} \\\\\n\\frac{CF}{2} &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\nCF &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073614 1069 "e-3187" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073614 1071 "![](images/fa/fa058bb3e90d8bb764c6fdb7369c183ffc67fcf815ade124ad956edddf908566.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The measure of $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$ is equal to $100^\\circ$\n2. The measure of $\\overset{\\frown}{ACB}$ is equal to $240^\\circ$\n3. The measure of $\\overset{\\frown}{ACB}$ is equal to $120^\\circ$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073614 1072 #uuid "c72a516a-88e3-5ec7-96d7-71c32c93bca1" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073614 1074 "Let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. The measure of the minor arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$ is equal to the measure of its central angle,\n$m\\overset{\\frown}{AB} = m\\angle{AOB}=120^\\circ$.\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. The arc $\\overset{\\frown}{ACB}$ represents the major arc of the circle, so it has the measure\n$m\\overset{\\frown}{ACB} = 360^\\circ - m\\angle{AOB} =360^\\circ - 120^\\circ =240^\\circ$.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073615 1069 "e-3188" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073615 1071 "![](images/3c/3c00024dd8bde37f9d1041218fdeb3e1149d5582c02f649cc46b0407fd601ee7.png)\n\nWhat is the measure, in degrees, of the central angle corresponding to the arc $\\overset{\\frown}{BC}$?" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073615 1072 #uuid "af77dcf5-5673-591f-a142-dc08b0aa7054" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073615 1074 "When an arc notation contains only two letters, it is always assumed to be a minor arc.\n\nSo here, $\\overset{\\frown}{BC}$ represents the minor arc of the circle with the central angle $\\angle{BOC}$.\n\nTherefore, its measure is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmBC^(⌢) &= m∠BOC \\\\\n&= m∠AOB + m∠AOC \\\\\n&= 90^{∘} + 45^{∘} \\\\\n&= 135^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073616 1069 "e-3192" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073616 1071 "![](images/cd/cd4c6e9ea5e1117b6fe999b37447d96c18e7fc59de2e0332c8f5c1edb27828e1.png)\n\nGiven that $m\\angle{DOA}=125^\\circ$, $m\\angle{AOB} = 95^\\circ$ and $m\\angle{COD}=90^\\circ$, find the measure of $\\angle{AOC}$?" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073616 1072 #uuid "a975b468-1318-5a60-ae85-80fbcf918a5b" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073616 1074 "Notice that\n\n$$\nm\\angle COD = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nm\\angle AOC = m\\angle AOB + m\\angle BOC\n$$\n\nSince all the angles in the diagram together make a full circle of $360^\\circ$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠DOA + (m∠AOB + m∠BOC) + m∠COD &= 360^{∘} \\\\\n125^{∘} + m∠AOC + 90^{∘} &= 360^{∘} \\\\\n215^{∘} + m∠AOC &= 360^{∘} \\\\\nm∠AOC &= 145^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073617 1069 "e-3194" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073617 1071 "![](images/36/36f66f7f56a7700b35827ba9668cfb3a10f06d37b1728b9f0da139ededde28f7.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $\\overline{AD}$, $\\overline{BE}$, and $\\overline{CF}$ are diameters. If $m\\angle{AOF=50^\\circ}$ and $m\\overset{\\frown}{BC}=3x-10^\\circ$, then what is the value of $x$?" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073617 1072 #uuid "8574192d-8b2c-513b-bad2-02204612cb74" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073617 1074 "From the diagram,\n\n$$\nm\\angle{EOD} = m\\angle{AOF} = 50^\\circ\n$$\n\nSince the measures of $\\angle{AOF}$, $\\angle{FOE}$ and $\\angle{EOD}$, add up to $180^\\circ$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠AOF + m∠FOE + m∠EOD &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n50^{∘} + m∠FOE + 50^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n100^{∘} + m∠FOE &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠FOE &= 80^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the angles $\\angle{FOE}$ and $\\angle{BOC}$ are vertical, we obtain\n\n$$\nm\\angle{BOC} = m\\angle{FOE} = 80^\\circ\n$$\n\nFinally, since the measure of the arc $\\overset{\\frown}{BC}$ is equal to the measure of the central angle $\\angle{BOC}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nBC^(⌢) &= m∠BOC \\\\\n3x - 10^{∘} &= m∠BOC \\\\\n3x - 10^{∘} &= 80^{∘} \\\\\n3x &= 90^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 30^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073618 1069 "e-3198" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073618 1071 "![](images/72/72020b5b4ea830a1e5b8e968bf85f6c8a926752f8ed664c207d5e1d43355aa55.png)\n\nThe area of the circle above is $81\\pi\\,\\textrm{ft}^2$, and the length of the arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$ is $4\\pi\\,\\textrm{ft}$. What is the measure of $\\angle{AOB}$?" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073618 1072 #uuid "d5c71b95-a1d5-5487-8eff-4d52ad99f901" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073618 1074 "The formula for the area of a circle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\pi r^2\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius. Using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A = 81\\pi\\,\\textrm{ft}^2\n$$\n\nwe can solve for $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= πr^{2} \\\\\n81π &= πr^{2} \\\\\n81π &= πr^{2} \\\\\n81 &= r^{2} \\\\\nr &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative value since the radius can't be negative.\n\nTherefore, the circumference is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC &= 2πr \\\\\n&= 2π \\cdot 9 \\\\\n&= 18π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the ratio of the length of $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$ to the circumference $C$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4\\pi}{18\\pi} = \\dfrac{2}{9}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sector is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{9}\n$$\n\nof the whole circle, and we obtain\n\n$$\nm\\angle{AOB} = 360^\\circ \\cdot\\dfrac{2}{9} = 80^\\circ\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073619 1069 "e-3199" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073619 1071 "Suppose that the numerical value of $\\mathcal{A}+ C$ is $80 \\pi$, where $\\mathcal{A}$ is the area of a circle and $C$ is the circumference. Find the diameter of the circle." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073619 1072 #uuid "8ef4fe9c-8233-571f-8ab1-8f5dd6050d6c" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073619 1074 "Let $r$ denote the radius of the circle. Then,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\pi r^2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nC = 2 \\pi r\n$$\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}+ C = 80 \\pi\n$$\n\nwe can now solve for $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA + C &= 80π \\\\\nπr^{2} + 2πr &= 80π \\\\\nπ(r^{2} + 2r) &= 80π \\\\\nπ(r^{2} + 2r) &= 80π \\\\\nr^{2} + 2r &= 80 \\\\\nr^{2} + 2r - 80 &= 0 \\\\\n(r + 10)(r - 8) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative value since the radius can't be negative. Therefore, $r=8$.\n\nFinally, the diameter of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= 2r \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 8 \\\\\n&= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073620 1069 "e-3201" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073620 1071 "Find the area of the shaded region below given that the radius of the circle is $4\\,\\textrm{cm}$ and the length of the arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$ is $\\pi\\,\\textrm{cm}$.\n\n![](images/fe/fefcba2faf90fdb9ca83d6295d91948b474cb0eb021d8d4a1aa3a4a4cd46973e.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073620 1072 #uuid "df10e1aa-fbd6-5b97-b2b2-96cd1d8789e9" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073620 1074 "First, we will find the fraction of the circle's area that corresponds to the shaded region. This is the same as the ratio of the arc length of the shaded region to the full circumference of the circle.\n\nFirst, let's find the circumference of the circle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC &= 2πr \\\\\n&= 2π \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&= 8πcm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the length of the arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$ is $\\pi \\,\\textrm{cm}$, so the ratio of the length of $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$ to the circumference $C$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{8\\pi} = \\dfrac 18\n$$\n\nThis means the central angle $\\angle{AOB}$ corresponding to the arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$ covers\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nof a full turn, which means that the area $\\mathcal A_S$ of our sector covers\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nof the area of the entire circle.\n\nTherefore, we can compute the area of the sector ${AOB}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{S} &= \\frac{1}{8}πr^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8}π \\cdot 4^{2} \\\\\n&= 2πcm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073621 1069 "e-3203" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073621 1071 "A baseball diamond has a length of $80$ yards from the home plate $H$ to the outfield fence $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$. The lines from the home plate to first base $A$ and from the home plate to third base $B$ form an angle of $90^\\circ$, as shown in the figure below. What is the area of the shaded region that makes up the outfield?\n\n![](images/71/718ef120a1e80fa4f4728150f1fbc0143edd3e66edfbd6be2a73227e7b87c34a.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073621 1072 #uuid "55d0b01b-9750-59ac-89bb-962974b83474" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073621 1074 "The central angle corresponding to the arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$ is $\\angle{AHB}$. Notice that\n\n$$\nk= \\dfrac{90^\\circ}{360^\\circ} = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ represents the proportion of a full circle corresponding to $\\angle AHB$.\n\nNow, from the problem statement, we can deduce that the length of the inner radius $\\overline{HF}$ is\n\n$$\nHF = 80-60 = 20 \\, \\textrm{yd}\n$$\n\nLet's find the area of the inner sector (the part that corresponds to the infield):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{inner} &= k \\cdot πr^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot π \\cdot 20^{2} \\\\\n&= 100πyd^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we find the area of the outer sector:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}_{\\textrm{outer}} = \\dfrac 1{4} \\cdot \\pi \\cdot 80^2 = 1\\, 600\\pi \\, \\textrm{yd}^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area of the region that makes up the outfield is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{shaded} &= A_{outer} - A_{inner} \\\\\n&= 1600π - 100π \\\\\n&= 1500πyd^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073622 1069 "e-3266" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073622 1071 "Express $\\sin 20^\\circ$ as a cofunction of a complementary angle." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073622 1072 #uuid "83830700-8d9b-5205-8f3e-764247162987" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073622 1074 "The cofunction of $\\sin x$ is $\\cos x$, and the sine of an angle is the cosine of its complement.\n\nThe angle complementary to $20^\\circ$ is\n\n$$\n90^\\circ-20^\\circ = 70^\\circ\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\sin 20^\\circ=\\cos(90^\\circ - 20^\\circ)=\\cos{70^\\circ}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073623 1069 "e-3268" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073623 1071 "Find a value of $x$ satisfying the equation $\\tan 35^\\circ = \\cot x$." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073623 1072 #uuid "320908eb-adaf-5516-9810-a4fae4dc8f38" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073623 1074 "Since the tangent of an angle equals the cotangent of its complement, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan 35^{∘} &= \\cot (90^{∘} - 35^{∘}) = \\cot 55^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=55^\\circ$ satisfies the given equation." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073624 1069 "e-3270" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073624 1071 "Express $\\cos \\left(\\theta - 15^\\circ \\right)$ as a cofunction of a complementary angle." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073624 1072 #uuid "27b53e09-978d-5d78-aa3f-ad9c780adfeb" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073624 1074 "We know that the cofunction of $\\cos\\theta$ is $\\sin\\theta$, and the angle complementary to $\\theta - 15^\\circ$ is\n\n$$\n90^\\circ-(\\theta - 15^\\circ) = 90^\\circ - \\theta + 15^\\circ = 105^\\circ - \\theta\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\cos \\left(\\theta - 15^\\circ \\right) = \\sin\\Bigl(90^\\circ-(\\theta - 15^\\circ)\\Bigr) = \\sin \\left(105^\\circ - \\theta \\right)\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073625 1069 "e-3271" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073625 1071 "Given that $m\\angle A=\\left(2x+10\\right)^\\circ$ and $m\\angle B=\\left(3x+30\\right)^\\circ$, solve $\\sin A = \\cos B$ for $x$. Suppose that both $\\angle A$ and $\\angle B$ are acute." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073625 1072 #uuid "bd79dabb-70aa-58d8-9273-f15fed5d65cd" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073625 1074 "If both angles are acute and\n\n$$\n\\sin A = \\cos B\n$$\n\nthen the angles $\\angle A$ and $\\angle B$ must be complementary, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} m\\angle A+m\\angle B &= 90^\\circ \\ \\left(2x+10\\right)^\\circ+ \\left(3x+30\\right)^\\circ &= 90^\\circ \\\\ (2x+10) + (3x+30) &= 90 \\\\ 5x+40 &= 90 \\\\ 5x&=50 \\\\ x &= 10. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073626 1069 "e-3315" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073626 1071 "Given that $x=0.3$ radians, what is $\\sin{x}$ rounded to $3$ decimal places?" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073626 1072 #uuid "2b0df0b9-3484-5408-aadc-01e756c43de7" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073626 1074 "Note the argument of the sine is given in radians. Using a calculator in \"radians\" mode, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\sin(0.3) = 0.295\\,520\\ldots \\approx 0.296\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073627 1069 "e-3317" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073627 1071 "Evaluate $\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)$ by converting the angle to degrees." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073627 1072 #uuid "b9829363-8cde-55fb-a1de-b6460ef451a1" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073627 1074 "First, we convert\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nradians to an equivalent measure in degrees. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi} 6\\right) \\left(\\dfrac {180^\\circ} {\\pi}\\right) = \\dfrac {180^\\circ} {6} = 30^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right) = \\sin 30^\\circ\n$$\n\nFinally, since we know that\n\n$$\n\\sin 30^\\circ = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right) = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073628 1069 "e-3318" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073628 1071 "Rounded to three decimal places, what is $\\arcsin(0.75)$ in radians?" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073628 1072 #uuid "55d74eac-00aa-54cc-8e1f-743e053cc599" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073628 1074 "Note that we need the answer in radians. Using a calculator in \"radians\" mode, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\arcsin(0.75) = 0.848\\,062\\ldots \\approx 0.848 \\, \\textrm{radians}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073629 1069 "e-3319" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073629 1071 "Is the following statement true or false?\n\n$\\sin30^\\circ \\:\\overset{\\large\\color{red}?}{=}\\: \\sin \\left(\\dfrac16 \\right)$." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073629 1072 #uuid "7eadc0f5-b50a-5e8e-9706-7f4fe0554abf" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073629 1074 "The statement is false. Converting $30^\\circ$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n30^\\circ= 30\\cdot \\dfrac{\\pi}{180} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\, \\textrm{radians}\n$$\n\nThe statement is false because\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\neq \\dfrac{1}6\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073630 1069 "e-3392" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073630 1071 "Consider the figure below, where $AC=7\\,\\textrm{cm}$ and $BC=24\\,\\textrm{cm}$. What is the radius of the circle?\n\n![](images/82/82a447a21f800858db389a39268a65816a93cf77af79aec0a34790a4a850edf9.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073630 1072 #uuid "a5f0bf69-41d6-5636-8193-bfff6ca00dfe" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073630 1074 "Notice that $\\triangle{ABC}$ is inscribed in the circle, where the side $\\overline{AB}$ is a diameter. Therefore,\n\n$$\nm\\angle{ACB}=90^\\circ\n$$\n\nSo, by the Pythagorean theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AC^{2} + BC^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{7^{2} + 24^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{625} \\\\\n&= 25cm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of the circle is\n\n$$\nOA = \\dfrac{AB}{2} = \\dfrac{25}{2} = 12.5 \\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073631 1069 "e-3393" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073631 1071 "![](images/ea/ea1c35ac11e351546072ef6ebe3bc2d117de097e7d61b1bd4530c51d89394ed7.png)\n\nThe radius of the above circle is $10\\,\\textrm{m}$ and $AC=12\\,\\textrm{m}$. What is the length of the chord $\\overline{BC}$?" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073631 1072 #uuid "057dd2ff-4548-5d72-8d14-16a704712dea" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073631 1074 "Notice that $\\triangle{ACB}$ is inscribed in the circle, where $\\overline{AB}$ is a diameter. Therefore, by Thales' theorem, we have that\n\n$$\nm\\angle{C}=90^\\circ\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/8f/8f4aa8dc2fe7952d5eb19da0f942c2dfd116019acfb597a4c055b1837b8f5cac.png)\n\nNow,\n\n$$\nAB=2AO=2 \\cdot 10 = 20 \\, \\textrm{m}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the Pythagorean theorem,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nBC^{2} &= AB^{2} - AC^{2} \\\\\n&= 20^{2} - 12^{2} \\\\\n&= 400 - 144 \\\\\n&= 256\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\nBC = \\sqrt{256} = 16\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073632 1069 "e-3397" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073632 1071 "In the diagram below, $m\\angle BAD=2x+10^{\\circ}$ and $m\\angle BCD=2x-30^{\\circ}$. What is the value of $x$?\n\n![](images/32/3206a27cbe514239bc1e61c11bee785be01c1ea1702612c02eee53b8d4eb5692.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073632 1072 #uuid "57b336c4-97ec-5a48-a0f2-c5a17cc07a9a" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073632 1074 "Notice that $ABCD$ is a quadrilateral inscribed in a circle. So, its opposite angles are supplementary.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠BAD + m∠BCD &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n(2x + 10^{∘}) + (2x - 30^{∘}) &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n4x - 20^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n4x &= 200^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 50^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073633 1069 "e-3399" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073633 1071 "![](images/45/45634578d6579f4a77444a7aefdd1e7b6c6ba44c4cf05d5508dd7b5f9b822038.png)\n\nFind the value of $x$." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073633 1072 #uuid "8c126a69-aa43-56ca-9a8c-a5d2fdd4b2b5" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073633 1074 "Notice that $\\triangle{ABC}$ is inscribed in the circle, where the side $\\overline{AB}$ is a diameter. Therefore, by Thales' theorem, we have\n\n$$\nm\\angle{ACB}=90^\\circ\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $\\triangle{ABC}$ is a right triangle, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠BAC + m∠ABC &= 90^{∘} \\\\\n(2x) + (2x + 10^{∘}) &= 90^{∘} \\\\\n4x + 10^{∘} &= 90^{∘} \\\\\n4x &= 80^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 20^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073634 1069 "e-3400" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073634 1071 "In the diagram below, $m\\overset{\\frown}{AB}=80^\\circ$ and $m\\overset{\\frown}{DA}=70^\\circ$. Find $m\\angle{BAD}$.\n\n![](images/dc/dca3799a0f0fae71439893acab29e14c4abb72c8cc8c3aac19d6b429f726534d.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073634 1072 #uuid "c843e7d6-3de3-545e-81de-856225eb50d7" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073634 1074 "From the picture,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmDAB^(⌢) &= mDA^(⌢) + mAB^(⌢) \\\\\n&= 70^{∘} + 80^{∘} \\\\\n&= 150^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\angle{BCD}$ is an inscribed angle that intercepts the arc $\\overset{\\frown}{DAB}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠BCD &= \\frac{1}{2}mDAB^(⌢) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 150^{∘} \\\\\n&= 75^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that $ABCD$ is a quadrilateral inscribed in a circle. So, its opposite angles are supplementary.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠BAD + m∠BCD &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠BAD + 75^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠BAD &= 105^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073635 1069 "e-3407" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073635 1071 "![](images/49/49a0853959863a147ae9c08633484ca870f00c00cf9d3ab23afe00baf35e1aa6.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $ABCD$ is an inscribed quadrilateral\n2. $m\\angle{P}+m\\angle{R} = m\\angle{Q}+m\\angle{S}=180^\\circ$\n3. Both pairs of opposite angles in $EFHG$ are supplementary" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073635 1072 #uuid "11dc9614-36fe-5c37-be83-3779321f2b07" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073635 1074 "Let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. $ABCD$ is not an inscribed quadrilateral since the vertex $B$ does not lie on the circle.\n- Statement II is false. If we assume that\n$m\\angle{P}+m\\angle{R} = m\\angle{Q}+m\\angle{S}=180^\\circ$,\nthen $PQRS$ must be an inscribed quadrilateral, which is not true since $S$ does not lie on the circle.\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $EFHG$ is an inscribed quadrilateral since all its vertices lie on the circle. Hence, both pairs of opposite angles must be supplementary.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073636 1069 "e-3408" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073636 1071 "![](images/9b/9b0578ca96bb3363cbc1e7e3a201598f5dd15e4979445ef5f32a0216bbc72546.png)\n\nGiven that $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{PA}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{PB}$ are tangents to the circle at $A$ and $B$, respectively, what is the value of $z$?" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073636 1072 #uuid "b76d078f-1776-5a57-a81e-7178855ba6fa" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073636 1074 "Since $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{PA}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{PB}$ are tangent to the circle, we have that\n\n- $m\\angle{OAP}=m\\angle{OBP}=90^\\circ$, and\n- $\\overline{OA} \\cong \\overline{OB}$ are radii of the circle.\n\n![](images/21/21a2e5a5f962fa6a1807f825713405b5fce804ed999be594c9b98742fdbafb4b.png)\n\nTherefore, $\\triangle{OAP}$ and $\\triangle{OBP}$ are congruent by HL (Hypotenuse-Leg):\n$\\triangle OAP \\cong \\triangle OBP$.\n\nAs a consequence, the tangent segments $\\overline{PB}$ and $\\overline{PA}$ are congruent, and we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nPB &= PA \\\\\n5z + 2 &= 12 \\\\\n5z &= 10 \\\\\nz &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073637 1069 "e-3415" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073637 1071 "![](images/d5/d581a53c772fb3b9a0786ed09b086949bac25d82fbef7e68ff0e47147e0896a8.png)\n\nThe circle above touches the sides of the triangle at the points $A_1$, $B_1$, and $C_1$. Find the perimeter of $\\triangle{ABC}$." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073637 1072 #uuid "562a42ad-dd85-56cd-bd24-b45a83e596f2" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073637 1074 "First, notice that\n\n$$\nCA_1 = CB - BA_1 = 12 - 5 = 7\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}$, $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BC}$, and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AC}$ are tangent to the circle, then we get three pairs of congruent tangent segments:\n$AB_1 \\cong AC_1, \\quad BA_1 \\cong BC_1, \\quad CA_1 \\cong CB_1$.\n\n![](images/70/701bfddfb3f7c64c971227efd6ea41dbe176e9f6240d1c0fe095c9eb15a3ddfd.png)\n\nAs a consequence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= AC_{1} + BC_{1} \\\\\n&= 11 + 5 \\\\\n&= 16cm, \\\\\nAC &= AB_{1} + CB_{1} \\\\\n&= 11 + 7 \\\\\n&= 18cm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required perimeter is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np &= AB + BC + CA \\\\\n&= 16 + 12 + 18 \\\\\n&= 46cm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073638 1069 "e-3419" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073638 1071 "![](images/d1/d19227e7e7f30a2f3044e31989663406274a9f6e2a2873491e087912ce8e7711.png)\n\nIn the picture above, $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BC}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BD}$ are tangents to the circle at $C$ and $D$, respectively. If $OD=3\\,\\textrm{cm}$, what is the value of $OB$?" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073638 1072 #uuid "4f987347-92ce-53e4-9982-8328abe6a6b7" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073638 1074 "Since $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BC}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BD}$ are tangent to the circle, then\n\n- $m\\angle{OCB}=m\\angle{ODB}=90^\\circ$, and\n- $\\overline{OC} \\cong \\overline{OD}$ are radii of the circle.\n\nTherefore, $\\triangle{OCB}$ and $\\triangle{ODB}$ are congruent by HL (Hypotenuse-Leg).\n\n![](images/87/875f6e33812c9f6f2a5356ee2c9723f8f3d9fccdc79ab5960cb339e4bd1e753e.png)\n\nAs a consequence, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠DBO &= m∠CBO \\\\\n&= \\frac{m∠DBC}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{60^{∘}}{2} \\\\\n&= 30^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\triangle{ODB}$ is a right $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle, and therefore its hypotenuse is twice the length of its shortest leg:\n\n$$\nOB = 2 \\cdot OD = 2 \\cdot 3 = 6 \\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073639 1069 "e-3437" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073639 1071 "![](images/67/67724b0961a24c9dfed33bfbd45118d7243dfca8ba5b923af4c73cd4d56b39f2.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\angle{ACB}$ intercepts the arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AC}$\n2. $\\angle{AOC}$ is an inscribed angle\n3. $\\angle{ACB}$ is an inscribed angle" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073639 1072 #uuid "070d576e-c2c1-559e-8b4a-cb2a81f83fd9" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073639 1074 "Let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. The angle $\\angle ACB$ intercepts the arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/4d/4dc2c677de8b1478402014383edd0360a471414f9384c8523494d3541285874e.png)\n\n- Statement II is false. The angle $\\angle{AOC}$ is a central angle of the circle, not an inscribed angle.\n- Statement III is true. Notice that $\\angle{ACB}$ is formed by two chords, and its vertex lies on the circumference. Hence, $\\angle{ACB}$ is an inscribed angle.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073640 1069 "e-3438" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073640 1071 "If $m\\overset{\\frown}{CD}= 110^\\circ,\\,$ then find the measure of $m\\angle CED$.\n\n![](images/11/11b4565ede23fefc7595669385087d78e7979aa499fc169daeabce6fb81012fe.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073640 1072 #uuid "d84c0a3e-76e2-5712-91de-fcd200051cfa" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073640 1074 "Since we're told that\n\n$$\nm\\overset{\\frown}{CD}= 110^\\circ,\\,\n$$\n\nwe can add this information to the diagram:\n\n![](images/45/452aacbec53807737e83d54045f8ab963d0505ab5ac55dcc341870f0456698fe.png)\n\nAccording to the inscribed angle theorem, the measure of an inscribed angle is half the measure of its intercepted arc. So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠CED &= \\frac{1}{2}mCD^(⌢) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 110^{∘} \\\\\n&= 55^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073641 1069 "e-4745" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073641 1071 "If a machine at a factory takes $6$ hours to complete $2$ batches of refractory bricks, how many hours does it take to complete $10$ batches?" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073641 1072 #uuid "a86fdc21-1c98-5929-8282-aed055b1b053" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073641 1074 "To find the number of hours it takes the machine to complete $10$ batches of refractory bricks, we first find the unit rate of hours per batch and then multiply the unit rate by $10$ batches.\n\nTo find the unit rate of hours per batch, we divide the number of hours, $6$, by the number of batches, $2$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 \\div 2 &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $3$ hours per batch. To find the number of hours it takes the machine to complete $10$ batches, we multiply the unit rate by $10$ batches and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3 \\cdot 10 &= 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it takes the machine $30$ hours to complete $10$ batches." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073642 1069 "e-4746" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073642 1071 "At a certain sports center, there are $5$ equal-size pools. If a pump can empty $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ of a pool in $15$ minutes, then how many minutes will it take the pump to empty all $5$ pools?" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073642 1072 #uuid "52c7bc18-eedc-504f-bbae-a501944834a0" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073642 1074 "To find the number of minutes that the pump takes to empty $5$ pools, we can first find the unit rate of minutes per pool, and then multiply the unit rate by $5$ pools.\n\nTo find the unit rate of minutes per pool, we divide the number of minutes, $15$, by the number of pools,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n15 \\div \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nTo divide by a fraction, we multiply by the fraction's reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n15 \\div \\frac{3}{2} &= 15 \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{15}{1} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{15 \\cdot 2}{1 \\cdot 3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{30}{3} \\\\\n&= 30 \\div 3 \\\\\n&= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $10$ minutes per pool. To find the number of minutes that the pump takes to empty $5$ pools, we multiply the unit rate by $5$ pools and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10 \\cdot 5 &= 50\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the pump will take $50$ minutes to empty $5$ pools." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073643 1069 "e-4747" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073643 1071 "Ava works in a coffee store. She earns a bonus of $\\dfrac 3 4$ of a dollar for each $\\dfrac 3 8$ liters of coffee sold. How much of a bonus will Ava earn if she sells $18$ liters of coffee?" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073643 1072 #uuid "4f61ec9c-9b87-5b25-9543-44b6043b7b4a" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073643 1074 "To find the number of dollars that Ava earns if she sells $18$ liters of coffee, we can first find the unit rate of dollars per liter, and then multiply the unit rate by $18$ liters.\n\nTo find the unit rate of dollars per liter, we divide the number of dollars,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nby the number of liters,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{8}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4} \\div \\dfrac{3}{8}\n$$\n\nDividing one fraction by another is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{4} \\div \\frac{3}{8} &= \\frac{3}{4} \\cdot \\frac{8}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 \\cdot 8}{4 \\cdot 3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{24}{12} \\\\\n&= 24 \\div 12 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $2$ dollars per liter. To find the number of dollars that Ava earns selling $18$ liters, we multiply the unit rate by $18$ liters and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 \\cdot 18 &= 36\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Ava earns $36$ dollars if she sells $18$ liters of coffee." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073644 1069 "e-5008" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073644 1071 "Calculate the quotient of $681\\div 32$ using the relationship between multiplication and division." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073644 1072 #uuid "dd620c8b-de65-5518-9764-85b60c9339a9" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073644 1074 "Using our knowledge of multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n32 \\cdot 20 &= 640 \\\\\n32 \\cdot 21 &= 640 + 32 = 672✓ \\\\\n32 \\cdot 22 &= 672 + 32 = 704\\cdot(too big)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $681\\div 32$ has a quotient of ${\\color{blue}{21}}$." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073645 1069 "e-5009" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073645 1071 "Calculate $99 \\div 14$ using the relationship between multiplication and division." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073645 1072 #uuid "b929de25-275f-5892-8447-390d9c2b27f3" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073645 1074 "Using our knowledge of multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n14 \\cdot 5 &= 70 \\\\\n14 \\cdot 6 &= 70 + 14 = 84 \\\\\n14 \\cdot 7 &= 84 + 14 = 98✓ \\\\\n14 \\cdot 8 &= 98 + 14 = 112\\cdot(too big)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $99 \\div 14$ has a whole number part of ${\\color{blue}{7}}$.\n\nWe now find the remainder:\n\n$$\n99 - 98 = {\\color{red}{1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n99 \\div 14 = {\\color{blue}{7}}\\,\\textrm{R}\\,{\\color{red}{1}}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073646 1069 "e-5010" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073646 1071 "A toy factory can assemble $460$ toy cars per day. If these cars are stored in boxes containing up to $15$ cars each, how many full boxes per day does the factory produce?" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073646 1072 #uuid "d2bfa6e9-c869-5365-87cb-38360f60c47b" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073646 1074 "To find how many boxes of $15$ the factory can fill, we need to find the quotient of $460 \\div 15$.\n\nUsing our knowledge of multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n15 \\cdot 30 &= 450✓ \\\\\n15 \\cdot 31 &= 450 + 15 = 465\\cdot(too big)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $465$ is greater than $460$, the factory can't fill $31$ boxes.\n\nTherefore, the factory can produce $\\color{blue}30$ full boxes of toy cars each day." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073647 1069 "e-5011" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073647 1071 "Sean and Martin brought cupcakes for a party. Sean brought $35$ cupcakes, and Martin brought $46$. If each plate can hold up to $11$ cupcakes, how many plates can be filled completely? And how many cupcakes will remain?" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073647 1072 #uuid "d074bd37-7c76-589a-a82a-41088eb302a8" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073647 1074 "The total number of cupcakes to be distributed is\n\n$$\n35+46=81\n$$\n\nTo calculate the number of plates that can be filled, we need to figure out $81\\div 11$.\n\nUsing our knowledge of multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n11 \\cdot 7 &= 77✓ \\\\\n11 \\cdot 8 &= 77 + 11 = 88\\cdot(too big)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $88$ is greater than $81$, Sean and Martin can't fill $8$ plates. Therefore, $7$ plates can be completely filled with cupcakes.\n\nSince $\\color{blue}7$ plates will carry $77$ cupcakes in total, the number of cupcakes that will remain is\n\n$$\n81 - 77 ={\\color{red}4}\n$$\n\nSo we conclude that $7$ plates can be filled completely, and $4$ cupcakes will remain." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073648 1069 "e-5171" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073648 1071 "Solve the equation $2x^3 - 6x^2 + 4x= 0$." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073648 1072 #uuid "35be9962-fbfd-57f5-906e-41660079063d" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073648 1074 "First, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $2x$. We can factor out the $2x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{3} - 6x^{2} + 4x &= 0 \\\\\n2x(x^{2} - 3x + 2) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $x^2-3x+2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x(x^{2} - 3x + 2) &= 0 \\\\\n2x(x - 1)(x - 2) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $x = 0$, $x = 1$, and $x = 2$." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073649 1069 "e-5172" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073649 1071 "Solve for $x$ where $x^3 - x^2 - x = 3x^2 + 4x$." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073649 1072 #uuid "ccefedb4-9e45-56c4-bb11-460ed8fe1793" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073649 1074 "First, let's rewrite the polynomial equation in the form $P_n(x)=0$. To do this, we subtract all the terms from the right-hand side so that the right-hand side becomes $0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{3} - x^{2} - x &= 3x^{2} + 4x \\\\\nx^{3} - 4x^{2} - x &= 4x \\\\\nx^{3} - 4x^{2} - 5x &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $x$. We can factor out the $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{3} - 4x^{2} - 5x &= 0 \\\\\nx(x^{2} - 4x - 5) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $x^2 - 4x - 5$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(x^{2} - 4x - 5) &= 0 \\\\\nx(x + 1)(x - 5) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $x = 0$, $x = -1$, and $x = 5$." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073650 1069 "e-5173" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073650 1071 "If $3x^5 + x^3 = 3x^3 - 5x^4$, then what are the values of $x$?" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073650 1072 #uuid "830e79ed-49ac-537f-aa67-9bfd063bb258" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073650 1074 "First, let's rewrite the polynomial equation in the form $P_n(x)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x^{5} + x^{3} &= 3x^{3} - 5x^{4} \\\\\n3x^{5} + 5x^{4} + x^{3} &= 3x^{3} \\\\\n3x^{5} + 5x^{4} - 2x^{3} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $x^3$. We can factor out the $x^3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x^{5} + 5x^{4} - 2x^{3} &= 0 \\\\\nx^{3}(3x^{2} + 5x - 2) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $3x^2+5x-2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{3}(3x^{2} + 5x - 2) &= 0 \\\\\nx^{3}(3x - 1)(x + 2) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $x = 0$,\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nand $x = -2$." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073651 1069 "e-5739" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073651 1071 "Solve the equation $4x^4-4x^2+1 = 0$." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073651 1072 #uuid "fa3958bc-59bc-5142-a898-8ca827182b3b" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073651 1074 "We let $y = x^2$ and substitute $y$ in the place of $x^2$. Then, we can solve for $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 4x^4-4x^2+1 &= 0 \\\\ 4\\left(x^2\\right)^2 - 4\\left(x^2\\right) +1 &= 0 \\\\ 4(y)^2 - 4(y) + 1 &= 0 \\\\ 4y^2 - 4y + 1 &= 0 \\\\ (2y-1)^2 &= 0 \\\\ 2y-1 &= 0 \\\\ y &= \\dfrac{1}{2} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging $x^2$ back in for $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x^2 &= \\dfrac 1 2 \\ \\sqrt{x^2} &= \\sqrt{\\dfrac 1 2} \\ \\vert x \\vert &= \\dfrac {\\sqrt{1}} {\\sqrt{2}} \\ \\vert x \\vert &= \\dfrac 1 {\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ x &= \\pm \\dfrac 1 {\\sqrt{2}}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx = \\pm \\dfrac 1 {\\sqrt{2}}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073652 1069 "e-5740" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073652 1071 "Solve for $n$ where $3n^4 - 6n^2 = n^2 - 2$." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073652 1072 #uuid "34e14c0e-1c21-5db0-a103-236478ba25fd" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073652 1074 "First, we move all of the terms to the left-hand side so that the right-hand side becomes zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 3n^4 - 6n^2 &= n^2 - 2 \\\\ 3n^4 - 7n^2 + 2 &= 0 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe let $m = n^2$ and substitute $m$ in the place of $n^2$. Then, we can solve for $m$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 3n^4 - 7n^2 + 2 &= 0 \\\\ 3\\left(n^2\\right)^2 - 7\\left(n^2\\right) + 2 &= 0 \\\\ 3m^2 - 7m + 2 &= 0 \\\\ (3m - 1)(m - 2) &= 0 \\\\ m &= \\dfrac 1 3, 2 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging $n^2$ back in for $m$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} n^2 &= \\dfrac 1 3 \\ \\sqrt{n^2} &= \\sqrt{\\dfrac 1 3} \\ \\vert n \\vert &= \\dfrac {\\sqrt{1}} {\\sqrt{3}} \\ \\vert n \\vert &= \\dfrac 1 {\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ n &= \\pm \\dfrac 1 {\\sqrt{3}}, \\ \\\\ n^2 &= 2 \\ \\sqrt{n^2} &= \\sqrt{2} \\ \\vert n \\vert &= \\sqrt{2} \\\\ n &= \\pm \\sqrt{2}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nn = \\pm \\dfrac 1 {\\sqrt{3}}, \\pm \\sqrt{2}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073653 1069 "e-5741" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073653 1071 "Find all real solutions of $x^4 - 2x^2 =8$." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073653 1072 #uuid "3cb5c982-d162-531c-be8d-4991a4cf1079" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073653 1074 "We let $t = x^2$ and substitute $t$ in the place of $x^2$. Then, we can solve for $t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{4} - 2x^{2} &= 8 \\\\\nx^{4} - 2x^{2} - 8 &= 0 \\\\\n(x^{2})^{2} - 2(x^{2}) - 8 &= 0 \\\\\nt^{2} - 2t - 8 &= 0 \\\\\n(t + 2)(t - 4) &= 0 \\\\\nt &= -2, 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging $x^2$ back in for $t$ we get two equations:\n\n$$\nx^{2} =-2, x^{2} = 4\n$$\n\nThe first equation $x^2 = -2$ has no real roots. However, the second equation can be solved:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} &= 4 \\\\\n\\sqrt{x^{2}} &= \\sqrt{4} \\\\\n\\mid x \\mid &= 2 \\\\\nx &= ± 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the real solutions are $x = \\pm 2$." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073654 1069 "e-7747" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073654 1071 "![](images/70/707fabf08d90a67f39a89c27b1bb12f475447d63a86ef0867fbae1fd0d80eaf9.png)\n\nThe triangle shown above is rotated by $90^{\\circ}$ counterclockwise about the origin and then reflected across the $x$-axis. What is the resulting triangle?" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073654 1072 #uuid "14fc5fcd-4aaa-5c46-a991-4703ceb0434c" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073654 1074 "Let's apply the given transformation.\n\n- Rotating the triangle by $90^{\\circ}$ counterclockwise about the origin gives the following:\n\n![](images/fa/fae3cc7c804b24b6d6c35a5910963377683c3249e4b3c8f1440cc2338265ddd7.png)\n\n- Next, we take the last result and reflect it across the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/ee/ee29eebc26c9a852b5f58cc0f544c8c713f0cdae043cff519d3974639c8cb48a.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073655 1069 "e-7757" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073655 1071 "Given the triangles $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle PQR$ shown below, find $m \\angle P$.\n\n![](images/ce/ce06df0e44dc7b7e6a5e286b1001adbc7d0ed848e71458aa7b26e1562ac7ae6c.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073655 1072 #uuid "0ab6d68f-25a5-58eb-bb52-97243ce5d6ba" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073655 1074 "From the diagram, $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle PQR$ are congruent by SSS (side-side-side). Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}∠B & ≅∠Q, \\\\ ∠C & ≅∠R\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince the sum of the measures of the angles in a triangle is $180^\\circ$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠P + m∠Q + m∠R &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠P + m∠B + m∠C &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠P + 57^{∘} + 49^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠P + 106^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠P &= 74^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073656 1069 "e-7793" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073656 1071 "The point $P$ below is rotated by $45^\\circ$ counterclockwise around the origin and then reflected across the $x$-axis. Which of the given points is the image of $P$ under this sequence of transformations?\n\n![](images/eb/eb4416a7d6a0d131ad6b535571001b61a50152c59459bf125f42dff2788411cd.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073656 1072 #uuid "0f05c408-7ea2-58d2-a8e3-663f819747d1" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073656 1074 "Let's apply the given transformations.\n\n- Rotating the point $P$ by $45^\\circ$ counterclockwise around the origin gives the point $A$, as shown below:\n\n![](images/53/53128ffb3404a6fb27b096896f744b57e143ec2c5d630ae7ef9d80c256bfb43c.png)\n\n- Reflecting the point $A$ in the $x$-axis gives the point $D$, as shown below:\n\n![](images/23/234d8273aea8cfc59810b44f575887de0effe09d7ab95657b4d8b76baf822a04.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $D$." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073657 1069 "e-7831" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073657 1071 "Given the points $A(1,2)$ and $B(16,2)$, find the coordinates of the point $C$ that lies on $\\overline{AB}$ such that $AC:CB=3:2$." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073657 1072 #uuid "ad6fb10e-d276-5fe2-90b5-21a7c9ea95ba" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073657 1074 "First, notice that the $y$-coordinates of $A$ and $B$ are the same. Therefore, the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on the horizontal line $y=2$. Consequently, the point $C$ also has $y=2$.\n\nNow, since $AC:CB=3:2$, we must have\n\n$$\nAC=\\dfrac{3}{5}AB\n$$\n\n![](images/40/40924b18a186dbeafb1f634044092d76a1e65ed50145d7e5ab9bb329eee0d262.png)\n\nTo get from point $A$ to point $C$, we can \"run\" from $A$ to $C$ horizontally.\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of $C$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{C} &= x_{A} + \\frac{3}{5}(x_{B} - x_{A}) \\\\\n&= 1 + \\frac{3}{5}(16 - 1) \\\\\n&= 1 + \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot 15 \\\\\n&= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $C$ has coordinates\n\n$$\n\\left(10,2 \\right)\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073658 1069 "e-7832" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073658 1071 "Given the points $A(2,1)$ and $B(6,9)$, find the coordinates of the point $C$ that partitions the segment $\\overline{AB}$ in the ratio $1:3$." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073658 1072 #uuid "04c5bd02-cd2c-56f2-864c-319bf0e4606a" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073658 1074 "Partitioning a line segment $\\overline{AB}$ into a ratio $a:b$ means we must\n\n- divide the segment into $a+b$ equal parts, and\n- find the point $C$ that is $a$ equal parts from $A$.\n\nIn our case, the ratio $1:3$ implies\n\n$$\nAC:CB = 1:3\n$$\n\nThis means\n\n- there are $1+3 = 4$ equal parts, and\n- we want to find the point $C$ that is $1$ equal part from $A$. So, we have\n$AC= \\dfrac{1}{4}AB$.\n\nNext, consider another point $X$ that is horizontal with $A$ and directly below $C$, as shown in the diagram.\n\n![](images/29/29b01b5cc8cca113a14c16f95626faadb70993bbb42fab7fd77f011ecf0de734.png)\n\nTo get from point $A$ to point $C$, we can \"run\" horizontally from $A$ to $X$ and then \"rise\" vertically from $X$ to $C$:\n\n- The $x$-coordinate of $C$ is\n$x_{C}|= x_{A} + \\frac{1}{4}(x_{B} - x_{A}); = 2 + \\frac{1}{4}(6 - 2); = 2 + 1; = 3$.\n- The $y$-coordinate of $C$ is\n$y_{C}|= y_{A} + \\frac{1}{4}(y_{B} - y_{A}); = 1 + \\frac{1}{4}(9 - 1); = 1 + 2; = 3$.\n\nTherefore, $C$ has coordinates\n\n$$\n\\left(3, 3 \\right)\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073659 1069 "e-7878" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073659 1071 "Given the points $A(1,2)$ and $B(1,11)$, find the coordinates of the point $C$ that lies on $\\overline{AB}$ such that $AC:CB=2:1$." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073659 1072 #uuid "73576fff-7617-5a54-858c-c5f174ee5977" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073659 1074 "First, notice that the $x$-coordinates of $A$ and $B$ are the same. Therefore, the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on the vertical line $x=1$. Consequently, the point $C$ also has $x=1$.\n\nNotice that since $AC:CB=2:1$, we must have\n\n$$\nAC=\\dfrac{2}{3}AB\n$$\n\n![](images/02/0280b034fd9898a41e7695c0f7ff45fba78fe4ee920f55e521aa1d0992e21dba.png)\n\nTo get from point $A$ to point $C$, we can \"rise\" from $A$ to $C$ vertically.\nSo, the $y$-coordinate of $C$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{C} &= y_{A} + \\frac{2}{3}(y_{B} - y_{A}) \\\\\n&= 2 + \\frac{2}{3}(11 - 2) \\\\\n&= 2 + \\frac{2}{3} \\cdot 9 \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $C$ has coordinates\n\n$$\n\\left(1, 8\\right)\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073660 1069 "e-7899" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073660 1071 "Given the circle depicted below, what is the value of $x$?\n\n![](images/c6/c64a1cbe7131dfa2cb7961d64ed7d260f7e0c365cebbf294d748464db12ca57e.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073660 1072 #uuid "712debf1-d111-5d33-90e1-1295db989852" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073660 1074 "Since the inscribed angles $\\angle FDC$ and $\\angle FEC$ intercept the same arc $\\overset{\\frown}{FC}$, their measures are the same.\n\n![](images/da/da658aa1307393d1539708966b98fdb16ad47e76817a6a50602c4e2dc3db233a.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠FDC &= m∠FEC \\\\\n3x + 10^{∘} &= 40^{∘} \\\\\n3x &= 30^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 10^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073661 1069 "e-7915" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073661 1071 "![](images/39/39331a3780398bd3d8676c353a5ecc52fef5f0c41f7555d3f75e6c47456503ca.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $m \\angle D = 65^\\circ$ and $m \\angle A = 3x + 2^\\circ$. Find the value of $x$." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073661 1072 #uuid "2efbc9bc-b360-5302-bee9-26cde035d7a5" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073661 1074 "Notice that $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle DEF$ are congruent by ASA since we have the following pairs of congruent angles and included sides:\n\n- $\\overline{BC} \\cong \\overline{EF}$\n- $\\angle{B} \\cong \\angle{E}$\n- $\\angle{C} \\cong \\angle{F}$\n\nTherefore, all the corresponding angles of the triangles must be congruent. In particular, $\\angle A \\cong \\angle D$. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠A &= m∠D \\\\\n3x + 2^{∘} &= 65^{∘} \\\\\n3x &= 63^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 21^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073662 1069 "e-7916" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073662 1071 "A rectangle $ABCD$ is translated by $4$ units upward and then reflected over the $y$-axis. If the resulting quadrilateral is $PQRS$, which of the following statements must be true?\n\n1. $ABCD \\sim PQRS$\n2. $\\angle A \\cong \\angle P$\n3. $CD=RS$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073662 1072 #uuid "f89ebcc9-96b7-5d6b-b844-4a9328f2ab6b" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073662 1074 "A similarity transformation is any transformation made up of rigid motions (rotations, reflections, and translations) and dilations.\n\nA similarity transformation will map any polygon to a similar polygon.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We are given a combination of a translation and a reflection. As a result, we obtain that $ABCD \\sim PQRS$.\n- Statement II is true. Since $ABCD \\sim PQRS$, all corresponding angles of the rectangle must be congruent. In particular, $\\angle A \\cong \\angle P$.\n- Statement III is true. Since our combined transformation consists of rigid motions only (namely, reflection and translation), the distance between all points is preserved under the transformation. Therefore, we obtain that $CD = RS$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073663 1069 "e-8107" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073663 1071 "From top to bottom, what are the missing digits in the following long division problem?\n\n| $1$$1$$5$$4$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$0$$6$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$0$$4$$2$$3$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$0$$3$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073663 1072 #uuid "4e4c3088-724e-56c3-bb7b-14df1eb1327c" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073663 1074 "We start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $4$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the hundreds:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$4$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}4} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}6$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}4} \\div 4 = {\\color{blue}1}$Write over hundreds: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $4 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 4$Subtract: ${\\color{red}4} - 4 = 0$Bring down: $6$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the tens:\n\n| $1$$\\color{blue}1$$4$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$\\color{red}0$$\\color{red}6$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$0$$4$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$2$$\\color{lightgray}3$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}6} \\div 4 = {\\color{blue}1}\\,\\text{R}\\,2$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $4 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 4$Subtract: ${\\color{red}6} - 4 = 2$Bring down: $3$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, we consider the ones:\n\n| $1$$1$$\\color{blue}5$$4$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$0$$6$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$0$$4$$\\color{red}2$$\\color{red}3$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$0$$\\fbox{3}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}23} \\div 4 = {\\color{blue}5}\\,\\text{R}\\,3$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}5$Multiply: $4 \\times {\\color{blue}5} = 20$Subtract: ${\\color{red}23} - 20 = \\fbox{3}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $463$, so the division is done. However, we still have a remainder of $3$, so we include this in our final answer.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n463 \\div 4 = 115\\,\\text{R}\\,3\n$$\n\nThe missing digits are $1,6$ and $3$.\n\n| $1$$\\fbox{1}$$5$$4$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$0$$\\fbox{6}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$0$$4$$2$$3$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$0$$\\fbox{3}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073664 1069 "e-8139" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073664 1071 "What ratio describes the number of rhombuses to the number of triangles?\n\n![](images/49/49a799c1ec4b9e7caadd4af8714aa1133f33e7012b76083e9b241d05b0d2fee7.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073664 1072 #uuid "b3b354ef-26fc-55dc-acdd-4b28c5f273ef" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073664 1074 "First, we count the number of rhombuses. We see that there are $2$ rhombuses.\n\nThen, we count the number of triangles. We see that there are $3$ triangles.\n\nSo the ratio of rhombuses to triangles is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073665 1069 "e-8660" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073665 1071 "![](images/9f/9f7a8570aa5605fb598d10bfa35a571308b613229d1fedf2870a2e447a01c051.png)\n\nWhat is the length of $\\overline{AB}$?" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073665 1072 #uuid "2f8f7df9-4fca-5e7c-b82e-5c4f0db98ac2" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073665 1074 "The distance $d$ between the points $A(x_1,y_1,z_1)$ and $B(x_2,y_2,z_2)$ is given by\n\n$$\nd= \\sqrt{(x_2-x_1)^2 + (y_2-y_1)^2 + (z_2-z_1)^2}\n$$\n\nFinding the length of $\\overline{AB}$ is equivalent to finding the distance between $A$ and $B$.\n\nSince $A$ and $B$ have coordinates $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ respectively, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{(2 - 3)^{2} + (4 - 2)^{2} + (4 - 6)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-1)^{2} + 2^{2} + (-2)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{9} \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073666 1069 "e-8661" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073666 1071 "What is the distance between the points $A(2,5,3)$ and $B(-2,1,7)$?" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073666 1072 #uuid "5af72905-b5d5-5ac7-a43a-80e81b48c9aa" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073666 1074 "The distance $d$ between the points $A(x_1,y_1,z_1)$ and $B(x_2,y_2,z_2)$ is given by\n\n$$\nd = \\sqrt{(x_2-x_1)^2 + (y_2-y_1)^2 + (z_2-z_1)^2}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A\\underbrace{(2,5,3)}_{(x_1,y_1,z_1)}$ and $B\\underbrace{(-2,1,7)}_{(x_2,y_2,z_2)}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\sqrt{(-2 - 2)^{2} + (1 - 5)^{2} + (7 - 3)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-4)^{2} + (-4)^{2} + 4^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{16 + 16 + 16} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{48} \\\\\n&= 4\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073667 1069 "e-8662" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073667 1071 "Consider the points $A(p, 3p, p)$ and $B(3p, p, 0)$. Given that the distance between $A$ and $B$ is $6$, find the possible values of $p$." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073667 1072 #uuid "51e6d92d-2c73-5f11-9599-057b00085411" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073667 1074 "The distance $d$ between the points $A(x_1,y_1,z_1)$ and $B(x_2,y_2,z_2)$ is given by\n\n$$\nd= \\sqrt{(x_2-x_1)^2 + (y_2-y_1)^2 + (z_2-z_1)^2}\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula for $A\\underbrace{(p, 3p, p)}_{(x_1,y_1,z_1)}$ and $B\\underbrace{(3p, p, 0)}_{(x_2,y_2,z_2)}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\sqrt{(3p - p)^{2} + (p - 3p)^{2} + (0 - p)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(2p)^{2} + (-2p)^{2} + (-p)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4p^{2} + 4p^{2} + p^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{9p^{2}} \\\\\n&= 3 \\mid p \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $d= 6$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3 \\mid p \\mid &= 6 \\\\\n\\mid p \\mid &= 2 \\\\\np &= ± 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073668 1069 "e-8962" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073668 1071 "Find the measure of $\\angle{EBC}$.\n\n![](images/cf/cfbf4147313b6f9f0f5fd9c9a86481c2ead89394ee797ad5df5e4c98ac4fda68.png)" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073668 1072 #uuid "ed3282e2-fdd8-57ec-b04d-c813e0e92ac8" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073668 1074 "First, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠DAB &= m∠DAE + m∠EAB \\\\\n&= 21^{∘} + 45^{∘} \\\\\n&= 66^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\triangle{ADB}$ and $\\triangle{ACB}$ are inscribed in the circle, where the side $\\overline{AB}$ is a diameter. Therefore,\n\n$$\nm\\angle{ADB}=m\\angle{ACB}=90^\\circ\n$$\n\n- For $\\triangle{ADB}$, we obtain\n$m∠ABD|= 90^{∘} - m∠DAB; = 90^{∘} - 66^{∘}; = 24^{∘}$.\n- For $\\triangle{ACB}$, we have\n$m∠ABC|= 90^{∘} - m∠CAB; = 90^{∘} - 45^{∘}; = 45^{∘}$.\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠EBC &= m∠ABC - m∠ABD \\\\\n&= 45^{∘} - 24^{∘} \\\\\n&= 21^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073669 1069 "e-9728" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073669 1071 "Simplify $2\\sqrt[3]{5} +5 \\sqrt{10} -3 \\sqrt[3]{40}$." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073669 1072 #uuid "e5202e06-b6a8-5d74-a395-fe7c56b8e9bb" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073669 1074 "First, we use the product rule for radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt[3]{40} &= \\sqrt[3]{8 \\cdot 5} = \\sqrt[3]{8} \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{5} = 2\\sqrt[3]{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\sqrt[3]{5} + 5\\sqrt{10} - 3\\sqrt[3]{40} &=  \\\\\n2\\sqrt[3]{5} + 5\\sqrt{10} - 3(2\\sqrt[3]{5}) &=  \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt[3]{5} + 5\\sqrt{10} - 6\\sqrt[3]{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can combine the two $\\sqrt[3]{5}$ terms together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\sqrt[3]{5} + 5\\sqrt{10} - 6\\sqrt[3]{5} &=  \\\\\n5\\sqrt{10} + (2\\sqrt[3]{5} - 6\\sqrt[3]{5}) &=  \\\\\n5\\sqrt{10} + (2 - 6)\\sqrt[3]{5} &=  \\\\\n&= 5\\sqrt{10} - 4\\sqrt[3]{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073670 1069 "e-9729" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073670 1071 "Simplify the expression $3\\sqrt{80} -8\\sqrt{5}$." 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073670 1072 #uuid "c1f1d037-45a6-5e72-a735-0c0512290120" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [17592186073670 1074 "First, we use the product rule for radicals to simplify $\\sqrt{80}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{80} &= \\sqrt{16 \\cdot 5} = \\sqrt{16} \\cdot \\sqrt{5} = 4\\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3\\sqrt{80} - 8\\sqrt{5} &=  \\\\\n3(4\\sqrt{5}) - 8\\sqrt{5} &=  \\\\\n&= 12\\sqrt{5} - 8\\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can combine the two $\\sqrt{5}$ terms together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12\\sqrt{5} - 8\\sqrt{5} &=  \\\\\n(12 - 8)\\sqrt{5} &=  \\\\\n&= 4\\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533347 17592186045585 true] [1